LM.SEARCH_INDEX = {"version":1,"records":[{"id":"page:a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation","group":"pages","title":"Alphabet, pronunciation and stress","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Spanish is read the way it is written. Learn the few letters that differ from English, where the stress falls, and ¿ ¡.","text":"Spanish spelling is regular: once you know the letters, you can read any word aloud and usually write a word you hear. Compare English though, through and tough. Spanish has five clear vowels, a few letters that differ from English, and simple stress rules. How it works Vowels. There are five, and each has one short, clear sound. They never change into \"uh\" as in English banana. Letter Sounds like Example a a in father casa (house) e e in bed mesa (table) i ee in see, but short sí (yes) o o in go, but short, without the w glide at the end ocho (eight) u oo in food, short uno (one) Letters that differ from English. Letter How to say it Example h silent hola, hotel j, and g before e / i a breathy sound from the throat, like a strong h José, gente ñ ny as in canyon España, mañana ll, y y as in yes (in some regions closer to j in jam; in Argentina and Uruguay like sh) calle, yo r between vowels one quick tap, like tt in American better pero (but) rr, and r at the start of a word or after n, l, s a rolled r perro (dog), rojo, Enrique b, v the same sound vaso, bar c before e / i, z th in think in most of Spain; s in Latin America, the Canary Islands and parts of southern Spain cine, zona c before a / o / u, g before a / o / u k; g in go casa, gracias qu k (the u is silent) queso Stress. Every word has one strong syllable. Three rules find it: A word ending in a vowel, n or s is stressed on the second-to-last syllable: CA-sa, HA-blan, LU-nes. A word ending in any other consonant is stressed on the last syllable: ho-TEL, ciu-DAD. A written accent breaks the rules and shows the stressed syllable: ca-FÉ, te-LÉ-fo-no, Jo-SÉ. Questions and exclamations have a mark at the start, upside down: ¿Cómo te llamas? ¡Hola! When to use it Reading a new word. Say each letter, then put the stress where the rules say. Spelling your name. People will ask ¿Cómo se escribe? (How do you spell it?). The letter names: a, be, ce, de, e, efe, ge, hache, i, jota, ka, ele, eme, ene, eñe, o, pe, cu, erre, ese, te, u, uve (Spain) / ve (Latin America), uve doble (Spain) / doble ve or doble u (Latin America), equis, ye, zeta. Writing. A missing accent or ñ can change the meaning: papa (potato, Latin America) and papá (dad); ano (anus) and año (year). Examples Spanish English hola hello (the h is silent) España Spain la calle the street el perro the dog (rolled rr) pero but (one tap) el teléfono the telephone (te-LÉ-fo-no) ¿Cómo te llamas? What's your name? Good to know ch sounds as in church: chocolate. Common mistakes ✗ Como te llamas? → ✓ ¿Cómo te llamas? — a question needs ¿ at the start, and question words have an accent. ✗ Espana → ✓ España — n and ñ are two different letters. ✗ pero (for \"dog\") → ✓ perro — one r is a tap; rr is a roll, and it is a different word. ✗ Jose → ✓ José — the name is stressed on the last syllable, and the accent shows it. Practice Where is the stress? Write the stressed syllable. mesa hotel café hablan Write the word correctly. Espana (Spain) el pero (the dog) Como te llamas? (What's your name?) ola (hello) Answers ME-sa (ends in a vowel) ho-TEL (ends in l) ca-FÉ (written accent) HA-blan (ends in n) España el perro ¿Cómo te llamas? hola","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-alphabet-pronunciation"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-greetings-introductions","group":"pages","title":"Greet people, say goodbye and introduce yourself","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Say hello at any time of day, give your name, ask someone's name, react politely and say goodbye.","text":"These are the first sentences you will use, and you can learn them as fixed phrases before you know the grammar inside them. One difference from English matters from day one: Spanish has two ways to say \"you\". Use tú with friends, family, children and people your age; use usted with older people, strangers in formal situations, and customers or senior people at work. Many phrases below come in both versions. Key phrases Saying hello Spanish English Hola. Hi. / Hello. (any time, any person) Buenos días. Good morning. (until noon in Latin America; until lunch, about 2 p.m., in Spain) Buenas tardes. Good afternoon. / Good evening. (until it gets dark) Buenas noches. Good evening. / Good night. (after dark, also to say goodbye) ¿Qué tal? How's it going? (very common, friendly) ¿Cómo estás? / ¿Cómo está usted? How are you? (informal / formal) Bien, gracias. ¿Y tú? / ¿Y usted? Fine, thanks. And you? Introducing yourself and others Spanish English Me llamo Ana. My name is Ana. Soy Ana. I'm Ana. ¿Cómo te llamas? What's your name? (informal) ¿Cómo se llama usted? What's your name? (formal) Este es Pedro. / Esta es Lucía. This is Pedro. / This is Lucía. Mucho gusto. Nice to meet you. Encantado. / Encantada. Pleased to meet you. (a man says encantado, a woman encantada) Igualmente. Likewise. Saying goodbye Spanish English Adiós. Goodbye. Hasta luego. See you later. (also for \"bye\" in a shop) Hasta mañana. See you tomorrow. Nos vemos. See you. (informal) Chao. Bye. (informal, common in Latin America) Dialogue At a language exchange, two people meet. Marta: ¡Hola! Me llamo Marta. ¿Y tú? (Hi! My name is Marta. And you?) Tom: Hola, Marta. Soy Tom. (Hi, Marta. I'm Tom.) Marta: Mucho gusto, Tom. (Nice to meet you, Tom.) Tom: Igualmente. ¿Qué tal? (Likewise. How's it going?) Marta: Muy bien, gracias. Mira, esta es Elena. (Very well, thanks. Look, this is Elena.) Elena: Hola, Tom. Encantada. (Hi, Tom. Pleased to meet you.) Tom: Encantado, Elena. (Pleased to meet you, Elena.) Marta: Bueno, hasta luego. (OK, see you later.) Tom and Elena: ¡Hasta luego! (See you later!) Culture note In Spain friends and new acquaintances often greet with two kisses, one on each cheek; in much of Latin America one kiss is usual. Men usually shake hands with each other. Buenas on its own is a friendly short form of buenos días or buenas tardes. When in doubt, start with usted. People will tell you if they prefer tú: Puedes tutearme (\"you can say tú to me\"). Common mistakes ✗ Buenos tardes. → ✓ Buenas tardes. — tarde and noche are feminine words, so it is buenas; only días takes buenos. ✗ Me llamo es Ana. → ✓ Me llamo Ana. — me llamo already means \"my name is\"; there is no extra verb. ✗ Encantado (said by a woman) → ✓ Encantada — the word agrees with the person speaking. ✗ ¿Cómo te llamas? (to an older stranger at an office) → ✓ ¿Cómo se llama usted? — use the formal version with people you would call \"sir\" or \"madam\". ✗ Bien, y tú? → ✓ Bien, ¿y tú? — a question inside a sentence still opens with ¿. Practice Choose the right greeting. It is 9 a.m. You walk into a bakery: (Buenos días / Buenas noches) It is 4 p.m.: (Buenas tardes / Buenos tardes) Complete the dialogue. —Hola, me Carlos. —Mucho gusto. Yo soy Rosa. —Encantada. —. (Likewise.) —¿Qué tal? —Bien, gracias. ¿Y ? (to a friend) Translate into Spanish. What's your name? (to your teacher's grandmother) See you tomorrow! Pleased to meet you. (said by a woman) Answers Buenos días Buenas tardes llamo Igualmente tú ¿Cómo se llama usted? / ¿Cómo se llama? ¡Hasta mañana! Encantada. / Mucho gusto.","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-greetings-introductions"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-subject-pronouns","group":"pages","title":"Subject pronouns; tú vs usted","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"I, you, he, she, we, they in Spanish; four words for \"you\"; and why Spanish often leaves the pronoun out.","text":"Subject pronouns are the words for \"I\", \"you\", \"she\" and so on. Spanish has more of them than English, for two reasons. First, \"you\" has a friendly form and a polite form, and a singular and a plural. Second, \"we\" and \"they\" show whether the group is all female. But Spanish uses them much less, because the verb ending already shows who is meant. How it works English Spanish Notes I yo no capital letter you (one friend) tú with an accent; tu without it means \"your\" he / she él / ella él with an accent; el without it means \"the\" you (one person, formal) usted often written Ud. we nosotros / nosotras nosotras: only women you (a group of friends) (Spain) vosotros / vosotras vosotras: only women they ellos / ellas ellas: only women you (a group) ustedes formal in Spain; all groups in Latin America. Often Uds. A mixed group takes the masculine form: Ana and Pedro are ellos; Ana, Rosa and Pedro are still ellos. Usted and ustedes mean \"you\", but they take the same verb form as él and ellos: ¿Cómo está usted? is \"How are you?\" There is no word for \"it\" as a subject: Es grande. (It is big.) When to use it Choose tú or usted. Tú for friends, family, children and people your age in relaxed settings. Usted for older people, customers, officials and anyone you would call \"sir\" or \"madam\". To a friend: ¿Y tú? To your new boss: ¿Y usted? Talk to a group. In Spain, vosotros for friends and ustedes for formal groups. In Latin America, ustedes for every group. Both are correct; use the one people use where you are. Leave the pronoun out. In a normal sentence the verb is enough: Me llamo Ana (not Yo me llamo Ana every time). Use the pronoun for contrast or emphasis. Yo me llamo Ana y él se llama Pedro. (I'm Ana, and he is Pedro.) —¿Quién es Marta? —Yo. (Me.) Examples Spanish English —Bien, gracias. ¿Y tú? Fine, thanks. And you? —Muy bien. ¿Y usted, señora? Very well. And you, madam? Él se llama Luis y ella se llama Rosa. He's called Luis and she's called Rosa. ¿Cómo se llama usted? What's your name? (formal) —¿Y vosotros? —Nosotros, bien. (Spain) \"And you guys?\" \"We're fine.\" —¿Y ustedes? —Nosotras, muy bien. \"And you?\" \"We're very well.\" (a group of women) Mucho gusto. Yo soy Pablo. Nice to meet you. I'm Pablo. Good to know In Argentina, Uruguay, Paraguay and much of Central America (and in parts of other countries) people say vos instead of tú with different verb forms (vos sos, vos tenés). You will understand it from context; this course uses tú. Common mistakes ✗ Yo me llamo Ana. Yo soy de Lima. Yo soy profesora. → ✓ Me llamo Ana. Soy de Lima. Soy profesora. — repeating yo in every sentence sounds unnatural. ✗ Usted te llamas Pedro. → ✓ Usted se llama Pedro. — usted takes the forms of él, not of tú. ✗ Ellos (for Ana and María) → ✓ Ellas — a group of only women is ellas, and nosotras for \"we\". ✗ Él es grande. (about a table) → ✓ Es grande. — there is no subject pronoun for \"it\". ✗ tu (for \"you\") → ✓ tú — without the accent, tu means \"your\". Practice Which pronoun? Write it in Spanish. you, to your best friend you, to an old man you meet in a museum they (Elena and Sofía) we (you and your brother; you are a woman) Translate into Spanish. And you? (to a group of friends in Mexico) And you? (to a group of friends in Madrid) He's called Pablo. What's your name? (to a hotel receptionist) Answers tú usted ellas nosotros ¿Y ustedes? ¿Y vosotros? / ¿Y vosotras? (¿Y ustedes? is also understood) Se llama Pablo. / Él se llama Pablo. ¿Cómo se llama usted? / ¿Cómo se llama?","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-subject-pronouns"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-ser","group":"pages","title":"ser: identity, origin and profession","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"The first \"to be\": say who you are, where you are from, what you do and what someone is like.","text":"English has one verb \"to be\". Spanish has two, ser and estar, and they are not interchangeable. This page is about ser, the verb for what something is: a name, a nationality, a job, a lasting quality. You will meet estar (where something is, how someone feels) in unit 3. Ser is irregular, so learn its six forms by heart. How it works Person ser English yo soy I am tú eres you are él, ella, usted es he / she is, you are (formal) nosotros, nosotras somos we are vosotros, vosotras (Spain) sois you are (plural) ellos, ellas, ustedes son they are, you are (plural) Negative: put no before the verb: No soy de aquí. (I'm not from here.) Question: the same words, with ¿ ? and rising intonation: ¿Eres de México? (Are you from Mexico?) Origin: ser + de + place: Soy de Chile. Jobs: no un / una before the job: Es médico. (He is a doctor.) When to use it Name and identity. Soy Marta. (I'm Marta.) Es mi amigo. (He is my friend.) Origin and nationality. Somos de Colombia. (We're from Colombia.) Es español. (He is Spanish.) Nationalities are written with a small letter. Job. Soy profesor. (I'm a teacher; a woman says profesora.) Son estudiantes. (They are students.) What someone or something is like (a lasting quality). Es inteligente. (She is smart.) Eres muy amable. (You are very kind.) Day and date. Hoy es lunes. (Today is Monday.) Examples Spanish English Soy Daniel. Soy de Canadá. I'm Daniel. I'm from Canada. ¿Eres estudiante? Are you a student? Mi madre es médica. My mother is a doctor. No somos de aquí. We're not from here. ¿De dónde es usted? Where are you from? (formal) Estos son Ana y Luis. Son de Madrid. This is Ana and Luis. They're from Madrid. Mi profesora es muy simpática. My teacher is very nice. ¿Sois hermanos? (Spain) Are you brothers? / Are you brother and sister? Good to know Es can mean \"he is\", \"she is\", \"it is\" and \"you are\" (formal). The context tells you which; add él, ella or usted only if it is not clear. With a job plus a description, the article comes back: Es un médico muy bueno. (He is a very good doctor.) For age Spanish uses tener, not ser: Tengo 25 años. See . Common mistakes ✗ Soy un profesor. → ✓ Soy profesor. — no un / una before a job on its own. ✗ Soy 20 años. → ✓ Tengo 20 años. — age is \"having years\" in Spanish. ✗ Yo es de Chile. → ✓ Yo soy de Chile. — each person has its own form: yo soy, él es. ✗ Es en Madrid. (where a person is) → ✓ Está en Madrid. — location of people and things takes estar; ser de is for origin (events are different; you will see them later). Practice Complete with the right form of ser. Yo de Perú. ¿Tú profesora? Nosotros amigos. Ellas de Italia. Usted muy amable. Translate into Spanish. I'm not from here. She is a nurse. Where are you from? (to a friend) Answers soy eres somos son es No soy de aquí. Es enfermera. / Ella es enfermera. ¿De dónde eres? / ¿De dónde eres tú?","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-ser"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-numbers-days-months","group":"pages","title":"Numbers 0–100, days of the week, months","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Count to one hundred, name the days and months, and say \"on Monday\" and \"in July\" the Spanish way.","text":"Numbers, days and months are everywhere: prices, phone numbers, plans. Two things surprise English speakers. Days and months are written with a small letter in Spanish. And you say \"on Monday\" without any word for \"on\": just el lunes. Words Numbers 0–20 Spanish English cero, uno, dos, tres, cuatro, cinco 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 seis, siete, ocho, nueve, diez 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 once, doce, trece, catorce, quince 11, 12, 13, 14, 15 dieciséis, diecisiete, dieciocho, diecinueve, veinte 16, 17, 18, 19, 20 Numbers 21–100 Spanish English veintiuno, veintidós, veintitrés 21, 22, 23 veinticuatro … veintinueve 24 … 29 treinta, treinta y uno, treinta y dos 30, 31, 32 cuarenta, cincuenta, sesenta 40, 50, 60 setenta, ochenta, noventa 70, 80, 90 noventa y nueve 99 cien 100 16–29 are one word: diecisiete, veintiocho. From 31 they are three words with y: cuarenta y cinco. Watch the accents: dieciséis, veintidós, veintitrés, veintiséis. Uno becomes un before a masculine noun and una before a feminine one: un euro, una persona, veintiún años. Days of the week (the week starts on Monday) Spanish English el lunes Monday el martes Tuesday el miércoles Wednesday el jueves Thursday el viernes Friday el sábado Saturday el domingo Sunday el fin de semana the weekend Months Spanish English enero, febrero, marzo January, February, March abril, mayo, junio April, May, June julio, agosto, septiembre July, August, September octubre, noviembre, diciembre October, November, December Word partners Spanish English Hoy es martes. Today is Tuesday. el lunes on Monday (this coming Monday) los lunes on Mondays (every Monday) de lunes a viernes from Monday to Friday el fin de semana on the weekend en julio in July en verano in summer el número de teléfono the phone number Mi número es seis, cinco, cero… My number is six, five, zero… ¿Cuánto es? —Son veinte euros. How much is it? —It's twenty euros. Good to know Phone numbers are read digit by digit or in pairs: 61 20 34 → sesenta y uno, veinte, treinta y cuatro. Septiembre is also spelled setiembre in some countries. Days ending in -s do not change in the plural: el lunes, los lunes. Sábado and domingo add -s: los sábados. Common mistakes ✗ Hoy es Lunes. → ✓ Hoy es lunes. — days and months take a small letter. ✗ en el lunes → ✓ el lunes — Spanish has no word for \"on\" with days. ✗ veinte y dos → ✓ veintidós — today 21–29 are written as one word. ✗ en Julio → ✓ en julio — months also take a small letter. Practice Write the number in words. 15 22 48 100 Translate into Spanish. on Saturday on Fridays in October Today is Wednesday. Answers quince veintidós cuarenta y ocho cien el sábado los viernes en octubre Hoy es miércoles.","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-numbers-days-months"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-gender-number","group":"pages","title":"Gender and number of nouns","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Every Spanish noun is masculine or feminine. Learn the endings that tell you which, and how to make plurals.","text":"In English a table is just \"it\". In Spanish every noun is masculine or feminine, even a table or an idea: el libro (the book) is masculine, la mesa (the table) is feminine. The gender has nothing to do with the thing itself, but it matters because the words around the noun change with it. Learn every new noun together with el or la. How it works Gender: most nouns show it in the ending. Ending Usually Examples -o masculine el libro, el vaso, el perro -a feminine la mesa, la casa, la playa -ción, -sión, -dad, -tad feminine la canción, la televisión, la ciudad, la libertad -or, -aje masculine el color, el viaje -e or a consonant (other than -or, -aje) either: learn it el coche, la noche; el hotel, la piel Common exceptions: el día, el mapa, el problema, el idioma, el sofá; la mano, la foto, la moto, la radio (el radio for the device in Mexico and parts of Latin America), la flor. People: many nouns change the ending (el amigo, la amiga; el profesor, la profesora). Nouns in -ista and -e often keep one form: el / la estudiante, el / la periodista. Number: making the plural. Singular ends in Add Examples a vowel -s libro → libros, coche → coches a consonant -es hotel → hoteles, ciudad → ciudades -z z → -ces lápiz → lápices, vez → veces el → los, la → las: los libros, las mesas. When a word stressed on the last syllable ends in -n or -s and adds -es, the written accent disappears: canción → canciones, inglés → ingleses. A stressed final vowel keeps it: sofá → sofás. Words in unstressed -s do not change: el lunes → los lunes. A group of men and women takes the masculine plural: los alumnos can be all boys or boys and girls. When to use it Every time you learn a noun. Learn la ciudad, not just ciudad. The article is your note of the gender. When you meet a new noun without an article. Look at the ending and guess with the table; you will be right most of the time. Talking about more than one. Dos hoteles, tres canciones, veinte días. Talking about mixed groups. Mis hermanos (mis = my) can mean \"my brothers\" or \"my brothers and sisters\". Examples Spanish English el día y la noche the day and the night la mano the hand el problema the problem las ciudades the cities los lápices the pencils dos canciones two songs los estudiantes the students (all men, or men and women) las estudiantes the students (all women) Good to know Some nouns change meaning with the gender: el capital (money) and la capital (the capital city). Most countries and cities are used without an article: Soy de Perú. Son de Madrid. Spanish has no neuter \"it\" nouns: everything is el or la. Common mistakes ✗ la problema → ✓ el problema — problema ends in -a but is masculine. ✗ el mano → ✓ la mano — mano ends in -o but is feminine. ✗ las ciudads → ✓ las ciudades — after a consonant the plural adds -es. ✗ los lápizs → ✓ los lápices — a final z becomes c before -es. Practice El or la? libro canción día foto Write the plural with the article. el hotel la ciudad el lápiz la canción Answers el la el la los hoteles las ciudades los lápices las canciones","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-gender-number"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-articles","group":"pages","title":"Definite and indefinite articles; al and del","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"el, la, los, las and un, una, unos, unas; when Spanish uses an article where English does not, and a + el = al, de + el = del.","text":"Spanish articles work like English \"the\" and \"a\", with two differences. They change with the gender and number of the noun, so there are four of each. And Spanish uses \"the\" more often than English: for things in general (la vida, life), with titles (el señor García) and with days (el lunes). If your language has no articles at all, this is the topic to practice most. How it works masculine feminine the (singular) el libro la mesa the (plural) los libros las mesas a / an un libro una mesa some unos libros unas mesas Two short forms. A (to) and de (of, from) join with el: a + el = al: el cine → Voy al cine. (I'm going to the movies.) de + el = del: el profesor → el libro del profesor (the teacher's book) Only with el: a la, de la, a los, de las do not change. A feminine noun that starts with a stressed a- takes el and un in the singular, to avoid two a sounds: el agua (water), un aula (a classroom). It is still feminine: el agua fría, las aguas. When to use it Something specific, or already mentioned: el, la, los, las. Es la casa de Ana. (It's Ana's house.) Something new, one of many: un, una. Es un problema. (It's a problem.) Things in general take el / la / los / las, where English uses no article: El café es caro aquí. (Coffee is expensive here.) Los niños son curiosos. (Children are curious.) Talking about a person with a title: el señor García, la doctora Ruiz. But not when you speak to the person: Buenos días, señor García. Days of the week: el lunes (on Monday), los lunes (on Mondays). No article with a job after ser (Es médico), with most countries (Soy de Perú), and for an unspecified amount of something: Hay pan y leche. (There's bread and milk.) Examples Spanish English Es un libro. Es el libro de Marta. It's a book. It's Marta's book. Las mesas son nuevas. The tables are new. El café es caro aquí. Coffee is expensive here. El señor García es de Chile. Mr. García is from Chile. Buenos días, señora Ruiz. Good morning, Mrs. Ruiz. Voy al cine el sábado. I'm going to the movies on Saturday. Es el coche del profesor. It's the teacher's car. Son unos amigos de México. They're friends from Mexico. Good to know Voy (I'm going) is a form of ir; you will learn it later. Here it only shows al. De + person shows possession, where English has 's: la casa de Ana (Ana's house). Common mistakes ✗ Coche es rojo. → ✓ El coche es rojo. — a singular noun as the subject needs an article. ✗ Voy a el cine. → ✓ Voy al cine. — a + el always joins into al. ✗ el libro de el profesor → ✓ el libro del profesor — de + el joins into del. ✗ El señor García, ¿cómo está? (speaking to him) → ✓ Señor García, ¿cómo está? — no article when you speak directly to someone. ✗ la agua → ✓ el agua — a feminine noun with a stressed a- takes el in the singular. Practice Write el, la, los, las, un or una. Es casa muy bonita. (a) niños son simpáticos. (the) ciudad es grande. (the) Hay problema. (a) Write al, del or de la. Voy cine. (el cine) Es el perro vecino. (el vecino) Es la casa profesora. (la profesora) Translate: Good afternoon, Mr. López. Answers una Los La un al del de la Buenas tardes, señor López.","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-articles"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-adjective-agreement","group":"pages","title":"Adjective agreement and position","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Spanish adjectives agree with the noun in gender and number, and they usually come after it: una casa blanca.","text":"An English adjective never changes: a white house, two white cars. A Spanish adjective follows its noun in two ways. It agrees: it takes the same gender and number as the noun. And it usually comes after the noun: una casa blanca is word for word \"a house white\". How it works Agreement. Adjectives have up to four forms. Adjective ends in masculine feminine masculine plural feminine plural -o alto alta altos altas -e grande grande grandes grandes a consonant azul azul azules azules nationality in a consonant español española españoles españolas -e and most consonant adjectives have one form for both genders: un coche grande, una casa grande; un libro fácil, una pregunta fácil. Nationalities in a consonant add -a for the feminine: inglés → inglesa, alemán → alemana. The accent disappears when a syllable is added. A mixed group takes the masculine plural: Juan y Ana son altos. Position. Most adjectives go after the noun: un coche rojo, una ciudad bonita, unos chicos simpáticos. These go before the noun: numbers and quantities: dos libros, mucho tiempo, pocas personas; bueno and malo often do, and they lose the -o before a masculine singular noun: un buen amigo, un mal día; grande before a singular noun becomes gran and means \"great, important\": un gran hombre (a great man), un hombre grande (a big man). When to use it Describing things. Es un coche rojo. (It's a red car.) Describing people with ser. Mi hermana es alta. Mis amigos son simpáticos. Saying where someone is from. Es mexicana. (She is Mexican.) Son españoles. (They are Spanish.) Nationalities take a small letter. Giving an opinion with muy. La casa es muy bonita. (The house is very pretty.) Muy never changes. Examples Spanish English una casa blanca a white house unos chicos simpáticos some nice boys (or boys and girls) Las calles son muy estrechas. The streets are very narrow. Mi profesora es colombiana. My teacher is Colombian. Es un libro fácil. It's an easy book. Los zapatos son azules. The shoes are blue. Es un buen restaurante. It's a good restaurant. Es un gran hombre. He's a great man. Good to know An adjective can also stand after ser, far from the noun, and it still agrees: La sopa es deliciosa. Los tomates son caros. Colors that are also nouns, like naranja (orange) and rosa (pink), usually do not change: unas flores naranja. Both flores naranja and flores naranjas are heard. Common mistakes ✗ la casa blanco → ✓ la casa blanca — the adjective takes the gender of the noun. ✗ un rojo coche → ✓ un coche rojo — colors and most adjectives come after the noun. ✗ las chicas simpáticos → ✓ las chicas simpáticas — gender and number both agree. ✗ Ella es inglés. → ✓ Ella es inglesa. — nationalities in a consonant have a feminine form. ✗ un bueno amigo → ✓ un buen amigo — bueno loses the -o before a masculine singular noun. Practice Write the right form of the adjective. una ciudad (bonito) unos coches (rojo) unas mesas (grande) María es (español) Put the words in order. coche / un / azul amigo / un / buen simpáticas / las / chicas / son Translate: It's a white house. Answers una ciudad bonita unos coches rojos unas mesas grandes María es española. un coche azul un buen amigo Las chicas son simpáticas. Es una casa blanca.","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-adjective-agreement"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-tener","group":"pages","title":"tener: possession, age and common expressions","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Tener means \"to have\". Spanish also uses it for age and for hunger, thirst, heat, cold and fear.","text":"Tener means \"to have\": Tengo un perro. (I have a dog.) Spanish uses it in more places than English does. Your age is something you \"have\": Tengo 30 años. And many feelings of the body are things you \"have\" too: Tengo hambre (I'm hungry, word for word \"I have hunger\"). Where English says \"I am…\", Spanish often says tengo. How it works Person tener English yo tengo I have tú tienes you have él, ella, usted tiene he / she has, you have (formal) nosotros, nosotras tenemos we have vosotros, vosotras (Spain) tenéis you have (plural) ellos, ellas, ustedes tienen they have, you have (plural) The yo form ends in -go: tengo. The stem changes to tie- in tienes, tiene and tienen, but not in tengo, tenemos and tenéis. Negative: No tengo hermanos. After no, a single countable thing often has no article: No tengo coche. (more natural than No tengo un coche) Question: ¿Tienes hermanos? (Do you have any brothers or sisters?) Expressions with tener (the noun after tener does not change): Spanish English tener hambre to be hungry tener sed to be thirsty tener calor / frío to be hot / cold (a person) tener sueño to be sleepy tener miedo to be afraid tener prisa to be in a hurry tener razón to be right To say \"very\", use mucho or mucha with the gender of the noun: mucho frío, mucho calor, but mucha hambre, mucha sed, mucha prisa. When to use it Possession. Tengo un coche nuevo. (I have a new car.) Family and friends. Tenemos dos hijos. (We have two children.) ¿Tienes novio? (Do you have a boyfriend?) Age. ¿Cuántos años tienes? (How old are you?) Tengo veintiún años. (I'm twenty-one.) How your body feels. Tengo frío. (I'm cold.) ¿Tienes hambre? (Are you hungry?) Agreeing with someone. Tienes razón. (You're right.) Examples Spanish English Tengo dos hermanos y una hermana. I have two brothers and a sister. ¿Cuántos años tiene usted? How old are you? (formal) Mi abuela tiene ochenta años. My grandmother is eighty. No tenemos coche. We don't have a car. ¿Tenéis hambre? (Spain) Are you (all) hungry? Los niños tienen sueño. The children are sleepy. Tengo mucho calor. I'm very hot. Tienes razón. You're right. Good to know Tengo calor is about how you feel. For the weather, Spanish says Hace calor (it's hot); you will learn it later. Estar caliente about a person can have a sexual meaning. Say tengo calor. Tener que + infinitive means \"to have to\": Tengo que trabajar. It comes in A2. Common mistakes ✗ Soy 25 años. → ✓ Tengo 25 años. — age takes tener, not ser. ✗ Estoy caliente. (I feel hot) → ✓ Tengo calor. — the body feeling is tener calor. ✗ Tengo mucho hambre. → ✓ Tengo mucha hambre. — hambre is feminine (el hambre, like el agua). ✗ Estoy hambre. → ✓ Tengo hambre. — hambre is a noun (hunger), so you \"have\" it. Practice Complete with the right form of tener. Yo un gato. ¿Cuántos años (tú)? Nosotros dos hijas. Ellos no coche. Translate into Spanish. I'm hungry. She is twenty years old. Are you cold? (to a friend) You're right. (formal) Answers tengo tienes tenemos tienen Tengo hambre. Tiene veinte años. / Ella tiene veinte años. ¿Tienes frío? Usted tiene razón. / Tiene razón.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-tener"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-personal-details","group":"pages","title":"Give and ask for personal details","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Ask and answer where you are from, how old you are, what you do, your phone number and e-mail, in both tú and usted.","text":"Forms, language courses, hotels and new friends all ask the same few questions. With ser and tener you can already answer most of them. Two things are different from English: \"What is your name / number?\" is ¿Cuál es…?, not ¿Qué es…?, and \"How old are you?\" uses tener: ¿Cuántos años tienes? Learn each question in two versions: with tú for people your age and with usted at an office or a reception desk. Key phrases Asking (tú / usted) Spanish English ¿Cómo te llamas? / ¿Cómo se llama? What's your name? ¿De dónde eres? / ¿De dónde es? Where are you from? ¿Cuántos años tienes? / ¿Cuántos años tiene? How old are you? ¿A qué te dedicas? / ¿A qué se dedica? What do you do (for a living)? ¿Cuál es tu número de teléfono? / ¿Cuál es su número? What's your phone number? ¿Cuál es tu correo electrónico? / ¿Cuál es su correo? What's your e-mail address? ¿Cómo se escribe? How do you spell it? Answering Spanish English Me llamo Ana García. My name is Ana García. Soy de Canadá. Soy canadiense. I'm from Canada. I'm Canadian. Tengo veintiocho años. I'm twenty-eight. Soy enfermera. / Soy estudiante. I'm a nurse. / I'm a student. Mi número es el 6 47 31 25 08. My number is 6 47 31 25 08. Es ana punto garcía arroba correo punto com. It's ana.garcia@correo.com. Con tilde / sin tilde. With / without an accent. Checking Spanish English ¿Perdón? ¿Puedes repetir? / ¿Puede repetir? Sorry? Can you repeat that? ¿Y tú? / ¿Y usted? And you? Dialogue At the reception desk of a language school. Receptionist: Buenos días. ¿Cómo se llama? (Good morning. What's your name?) Daniel: Daniel Wright. (Daniel Wright.) Receptionist: ¿Cómo se escribe Wright? (How do you spell Wright?) Daniel: Uve doble, erre, i, ge, hache, te. (W, R, I, G, H, T.) Receptionist: Gracias. ¿De dónde es? (Thank you. Where are you from?) Daniel: Soy de Toronto. Soy canadiense. (I'm from Toronto. I'm Canadian.) Receptionist: ¿Y cuántos años tiene? (And how old are you?) Daniel: Treinta y cuatro. (Thirty-four.) Receptionist: ¿Cuál es su número de teléfono? (What's your phone number?) Daniel: Es el seis, cuarenta y siete, treinta y uno, veinticinco, cero ocho. (It's 6 47 31 25 08.) Culture note In Spain phone numbers are often read in pairs or threes; in many Latin American countries digit by digit is more common. Both are understood. Spanish speakers have two family names, the father's and the mother's: Ana García López. On forms, apellidos means \"surnames\". @ is arroba, . is punto, - is guion and is guion bajo. W is uve doble (Spain), doble ve or doble u (Latin America). Common mistakes ✗ ¿Qué es tu número? → ✓ ¿Cuál es tu número? — \"what is\" + a piece of information is ¿cuál es…? ✗ ¿Cuántos años eres? → ✓ ¿Cuántos años tienes? — age takes tener. ✗ Soy Argentino. → ✓ Soy argentino. — nationalities take a small letter. ✗ Soy un estudiante. → ✓ Soy estudiante. — no article before a job or role on its own. Practice Write the question for each answer (use tú). —Me llamo Sofía. —Soy de Chile. —Tengo diecinueve años. —Soy camarero. Write the formal (usted) version. ¿De dónde eres? ¿Cuántos años tienes? ¿Cuál es tu número de teléfono? Translate: I'm a student and I'm twenty years old. Answers ¿Cómo te llamas? ¿De dónde eres? ¿Cuántos años tienes? ¿A qué te dedicas? ¿De dónde es usted? / ¿De dónde es? ¿Cuántos años tiene usted? / ¿Cuántos años tiene? ¿Cuál es su número de teléfono? Soy estudiante y tengo veinte años.","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-personal-details"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-family-people","group":"pages","title":"Family, describing people, nationalities, jobs","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Words for your family, how people look and what they are like, where they are from and what they do.","text":"With ser and tener you can now talk about the people in your life: Tengo una hermana. Es alta y muy simpática. Es médica. Remember three rules from earlier pages: adjectives and many nouns change for women (el médico, la médica), nationalities take a small letter, and there is no un / una before a job on its own. Words Family Spanish English el padre / la madre father / mother los padres parents (or fathers) el hermano / la hermana brother / sister los hermanos brothers, or brothers and sisters el hijo / la hija son / daughter los hijos children (sons and daughters) el abuelo / la abuela grandfather / grandmother el tío / la tía uncle / aunt el primo / la prima cousin (male / female) el marido / la mujer husband / wife el novio / la novia boyfriend / girlfriend Describing people Spanish English alto / bajo tall / short joven / mayor young / older, elderly rubio / moreno blond / dark-haired delgado slim simpático / antipático nice / unfriendly inteligente smart divertido fun tímido shy Nationalities (small letter) Spanish English español / española Spanish mexicano / mexicana Mexican colombiano / colombiana Colombian argentino / argentina Argentinian estadounidense American (from the US) canadiense Canadian inglés / inglesa English alemán / alemana German Jobs Spanish English el médico / la médica doctor el enfermero / la enfermera nurse el profesor / la profesora teacher el / la estudiante student el camarero / la camarera waiter / waitress (Spain; mesero in Mexico, mozo in Argentina) el ingeniero / la ingeniera engineer el / la policía police officer (la policía also means \"the police\") el abogado / la abogada lawyer Word partners Spanish English Tengo dos hermanos. I have two brothers (or a brother and a sister). Es hijo único. He's an only child. Mi hermana es alta y morena. My sister is tall and dark-haired. Mis padres son muy simpáticos. My parents are very nice. Mi abuelo es mayor y tiene noventa años. My grandfather is old; he's ninety. Es de Colombia, es colombiana. She's from Colombia; she's Colombian. Mi madre es profesora. My mother is a teacher. ¿Tienes novio? Do you have a boyfriend? Es muy divertido. He's a lot of fun. Good to know El marido / la mujer are used everywhere; el esposo / la esposa are also common, especially in Latin America. Mayor is the polite word for an old person. Viejo can sound rude about people. Americano can mean anyone from the Americas; estadounidense is only \"from the US\". Common mistakes ✗ mis parientes (meaning \"my parents\") → ✓ mis padres — parientes means \"relatives\". ✗ Es Mexicana. → ✓ Es mexicana. — nationalities take a small letter. ✗ Mi hermana es alto. → ✓ Mi hermana es alta. — the adjective agrees with hermana. ✗ Ella es inglés. → ✓ Ella es inglesa. — the feminine adds -a. Practice Write the female form. el abuelo el profesor Es español. Es alemán. Translate into Spanish. my parents I have a sister. He's tall and nice. She's a nurse. Answers la abuela la profesora Es española. Es alemana. mis padres Tengo una hermana. Es alto y simpático. Es enfermera.","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-family-people"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-present-regular","group":"pages","title":"Present tense of regular -ar, -er, -ir verbs","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Drop -ar, -er or -ir and add one ending per person. One pattern gives you hundreds of verbs.","text":"Every Spanish verb ends in -ar, -er or -ir in the dictionary: hablar (to speak), comer (to eat), vivir (to live). In the present tense you take off that ending and add a new one that shows who does the action. English adds only -s (I speak, she speaks); Spanish has six endings, one per person. The good news: once you know the pattern, it works for hundreds of regular verbs. How it works Person hablar (to speak) comer (to eat) vivir (to live) yo hablo como vivo tú hablas comes vives él, ella, usted habla come vive nosotros, nosotras hablamos comemos vivimos vosotros, vosotras (Spain) habláis coméis vivís ellos, ellas, ustedes hablan comen viven The yo form always ends in -o. -er and -ir verbs share every ending except nosotros and vosotros: comemos / vivimos, coméis / vivís. The pronoun is usually left out, because the ending shows the person: Hablo inglés. (I speak English.) Negative: no before the verb: No como carne. (I don't eat meat.) Question: the same verb with ¿ ?: ¿Hablas español? (Do you speak Spanish?) Spanish has no \"do / does\". Common regular verbs: -ar: hablar, trabajar (to work), estudiar (to study), escuchar (to listen), llegar (to arrive), comprar (to buy), tomar (to take, to drink). -er: comer, beber (to drink), leer (to read), aprender (to learn), comprender (to understand). -ir: vivir, escribir (to write), abrir (to open), recibir (to receive). When to use it Things that are true now. Vivo en Bogotá. (I live in Bogotá.) Habits and routines. Trabajo de lunes a viernes. (I work from Monday to Friday.) Actions happening right now. ¿Qué lees? (What are you reading?) English uses \"-ing\" here; Spanish can use the simple present. Near plans with a time word. Mañana trabajo hasta las seis. (Tomorrow I'm working until six.) Examples Spanish English Hablo inglés y un poco de español. I speak English and a little Spanish. ¿Dónde vives? Where do you live? Mi hermano trabaja en un banco. My brother works in a bank. No comemos carne. We don't eat meat. Los niños aprenden muy rápido. Children learn very fast. ¿Escribís en español? (Spain) Do you (all) write in Spanish? Mis padres viven en el centro. My parents live downtown. ¿Lees el periódico todos los días? Do you read the newspaper every day? Good to know Many verbs change their stem or have an irregular yo form (quiero, tengo, hago). They come in unit 4; see . Tomar is used for drinks and food in many places: Tomo un café. (I'm having a coffee.) The vosotros forms carry a written accent: habláis, coméis, vivís. Common mistakes ✗ Yo hablar español. → ✓ Hablo español. — the verb needs a personal ending; hablar is only the dictionary form. ✗ Él comes pescado. → ✓ Él come pescado. — -es is the tú ending; él takes come. ✗ Nosotros vivemos en Lima. → ✓ Nosotros vivimos en Lima. — -ir verbs end in -imos. ✗ Él come no carne. → ✓ Él no come carne. — no goes before the verb. Practice Complete with the right form. Yo (trabajar) en una tienda. ¿Tú (comer) pescado? Ana (vivir) en México. Nosotros (aprender) español. Ellos no (beber) café. Translate into Spanish. Do you speak English? (to a friend) We live downtown. I don't read the newspaper. Answers trabajo comes vive aprendemos beben ¿Hablas inglés? Vivimos en el centro. No leo el periódico.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-present-regular"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-questions-negation","group":"pages","title":"Question words and negation","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Ask with qué, quién, dónde, cuándo, cómo, cuánto, por qué; say no with no before the verb, and nunca, nada, nadie.","text":"Spanish questions are simpler than English ones: there is no \"do\" or \"does\". You keep the normal verb and add ¿ ? and a rising voice, or start with a question word. Question words carry a written accent when they ask (or exclaim). Negation is simple too: put no before the verb. One surprise for English speakers: Spanish uses a double negative, No como nada (\"I don't eat nothing\"), and it is correct. How it works Yes / no questions: the same words as a statement, with ¿ ? Often the subject goes after the verb. Hablas español. → ¿Hablas español? (Do you speak Spanish?) ¿Trabaja Ana aquí? / ¿Ana trabaja aquí? (Does Ana work here?) Both orders are correct. Question words (always with an accent): Spanish English Example ¿qué? what? ¿Qué comes? ¿quién? / ¿quiénes? who? ¿Quién es? ¿dónde? where? ¿Dónde vives? ¿de dónde? where … from? ¿De dónde eres? ¿cuándo? when? ¿Cuándo trabajas? ¿cómo? how? / what … like? ¿Cómo es tu casa? ¿cuánto? / ¿cuánta? / ¿cuántos? / ¿cuántas? how much? / how many? ¿Cuántos años tienes? ¿cuál? / ¿cuáles? which? / what? ¿Cuál es tu número? ¿por qué? why? ¿Por qué estudias español? The question word comes first; the verb comes right after it: ¿Dónde trabaja tu madre? A preposition goes before the question word: ¿De dónde eres? ¿Con quién vives? Cuánto agrees with the noun: ¿Cuántas hermanas tienes? The answer to ¿por qué? starts with porque (one word, no accent): Porque me gusta. (Because I like it.) Negation: no + verb: No hablo francés. (I don't speak French.) Short answers: —¿Eres de aquí? —No, no soy de aquí. The first no answers, the second one negates the verb. Negative words need no before the verb when they come after it: No como nunca carne. (I never eat meat.) No tengo nada. (I have nothing.) No hay nadie. (There is nobody.) Before the verb, they work alone: Nunca como carne. When to use it Asking for information. ¿Dónde está el baño? (Where is the bathroom?) Checking something. ¿Trabajas mañana? (Are you working tomorrow?) Saying what you don't do or have. No tengo perro. (I don't have a dog.) Saying \"never\", \"nothing\", \"nobody\". No trabajo nunca los domingos. (I never work on Sundays.) Examples Spanish English ¿Qué estudias? What do you study? ¿Quién es la chica de la foto? Who is the girl in the photo? ¿Cuándo llega el tren? When does the train arrive? ¿Cuántos hermanos tienes? How many brothers and sisters do you have? ¿Por qué no comes? —Porque no tengo hambre. Why aren't you eating? —Because I'm not hungry. No vivo aquí. I don't live here. No como nunca pescado. I never eat fish. No hay nadie en casa. There's nobody at home. Good to know ¿Qué? or ¿Cómo? can mean \"Sorry? What did you say?\". ¿Cómo? is more polite. ¿Qué es…? asks for a definition (¿Qué es una tortilla?); ¿Cuál es…? asks for one item of information (¿Cuál es tu dirección?). In exclamations the accent stays: ¡Qué bonito! (How pretty!) Common mistakes ✗ ¿Donde vives? → ✓ ¿Dónde vives? — question words carry a written accent. ✗ No veo algo. → ✓ No veo nada. — after no, use the negative word nada. ✗ Hablo no inglés. → ✓ No hablo inglés. — no goes right before the verb. ✗ ¿Por qué? —Por qué no tengo tiempo. → ✓ —Porque no tengo tiempo. — the answer uses porque, one word. Practice Choose the question word. ¿ vives? —En Lima. (Dónde / Cuándo) ¿ años tienes? —Veinte. (Cuántos / Cuántas) ¿ es tu profesor? —Es simpático. (Cómo / Qué) ¿ estudias español? —Porque me gusta. (Por qué / Porque) Make the sentences negative. Hablo alemán. Tengo algo. (→ nada) Hay alguien en casa. (→ nadie) Translate: I never work on Sundays. Answers Dónde Cuántos Cómo Por qué No hablo alemán. No tengo nada. No hay nadie en casa. Nunca trabajo los domingos. / No trabajo nunca los domingos.","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-questions-negation"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-estar-hay","group":"pages","title":"estar for location and states; hay vs está","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Estar says where something is and how someone feels. Hay says that something exists; está says where a known thing is.","text":"Estar is the second Spanish verb for \"to be\". You use it for where a person or thing is (Estoy en casa) and for how someone or something is at the moment (Estoy cansado). Next to it stands hay, \"there is / there are\", which says that something exists somewhere: Hay un banco en la plaza. English uses \"to be\" for all of this, so these three are worth learning together. How it works Person estar English yo estoy I am tú estás you are él, ella, usted está he / she is, you are (formal) nosotros, nosotras estamos we are vosotros, vosotras (Spain) estáis you are (plural) ellos, ellas, ustedes están they are, you are (plural) Watch the accents: estás, está, estáis, están. Esta without an accent means \"this\". Location: estar + en / aquí / allí …: Estamos en el hotel. States and feelings: estar + adjective, which agrees: Ana está cansada. Los niños están contentos. Hay (from the verb haber) has one form for singular and plural: Hay + a / an, numbers, or no article: Hay un parque. Hay dos bancos. Hay pan. Está / están + the, a name or my: El parque está allí. Mis llaves están en la mesa. Ask with ¿Hay…? when you don't know if something exists, and with ¿Dónde está…? when you know it does: ¿Hay un banco por aquí? (Is there a bank around here?) ¿Dónde está el banco? (Where is the bank?) When to use it Where someone or something is. ¿Dónde estás? —Estoy en el trabajo. (I'm at work.) How someone feels now. Estoy cansado. (I'm tired.) ¿Estás bien? (Are you OK?) The state of something. La puerta está abierta. (The door is open.) El café está frío. (The coffee is cold.) Asking and saying how someone is. ¿Cómo estás? —Muy bien, gracias. Saying what there is in a place: hay. En mi calle hay dos restaurantes. (There are two restaurants on my street.) Examples Spanish English Estoy en casa. I'm at home. ¿Dónde están las llaves? Where are the keys? Marta está enferma hoy. Marta is sick today. Estamos muy contentos. We're very happy. La tienda está cerrada. The store is closed. Hay un supermercado en la esquina. There's a supermarket on the corner. ¿Hay wifi en el hotel? Is there wifi in the hotel? No hay leche. There's no milk. Good to know Hay has no plural form: Hay una farmacia. Hay tres farmacias. Never hayn. Estar with bien / mal: Estoy bien. (I'm fine.) Bien never changes. The difference between ser and estar with adjectives (es aburrido / está aburrido) comes in . Common mistakes ✗ Soy en casa. → ✓ Estoy en casa. — where you are takes estar. ✗ Hay el banco en la plaza. → ✓ El banco está en la plaza. — with el, la use está; hay is for something new (hay un banco). ✗ Están dos farmacias en mi calle. → ✓ Hay dos farmacias en mi calle. — \"there are\" is hay. ✗ Ella esta cansada. → ✓ Ella está cansada. — esta without the accent means \"this\". ✗ Estoy cansado (said by a woman) → ✓ Estoy cansada. — the adjective agrees with the speaker. Practice Complete with the right form of estar. Yo en la oficina. ¿Dónde (tú)? Los niños cansados. Hay or está / están? un restaurante muy bueno cerca. El restaurante en la calle Mayor. ¿Dónde mis llaves? Translate into Spanish. Is there a pharmacy around here? We're at home. Answers estoy estás están Hay está están ¿Hay una farmacia por aquí? Estamos en casa.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-estar-hay"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-ser-vs-estar-basics","group":"pages","title":"ser vs estar: the basics","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Ser for what something is; estar for where it is and how it is now. Some adjectives change meaning with the verb.","text":"You now know both verbs for \"to be\". This page puts them side by side, because choosing between them is one of the first real challenges in Spanish. The short version: ser says what someone or something is (identity, origin, job, character), and estar says where it is and how it is at the moment. The same adjective can go with both verbs and change the meaning: Es aburrido (he is boring), Está aburrido (he is bored). How it works ser estar identity, name: Es Ana. location: Ana está en Madrid. origin: Es de Chile. feelings now: Está contenta. job: Es médica. states now: Está enferma. character, looks: Es simpática. Es alta. how it looks or tastes now: La sopa está rica. time and dates: Son las tres. Hoy es lunes. results of a change: La puerta está abierta. where an event takes place: La fiesta es en mi casa. bien / mal: Estoy bien. Some adjectives change meaning: with ser with estar Es aburrido. He is boring. Está aburrido. He is bored. Es listo. He is clever. Está listo. He is ready. Es rico. He is rich. Está rico. (food) It tastes good. Es malo. He is bad. Está malo. He is sick (mainly Spain; everywhere: está enfermo). / (food) It tastes bad. When to use it Ask \"what is it like in general?\" → ser. Mi ciudad es tranquila. (My city is quiet.) Ask \"how is it now?\" or \"where is it?\" → estar. Hoy la ciudad está tranquila. (The city is quiet today.) La ciudad está en el norte. Describing a person: ser for who they are, estar for how they feel. Pedro es tímido, pero hoy está contento. (Pedro is shy, but today he's happy.) Talking about food: ser for what it is (La paella es un plato español), estar for how it tastes now (La paella está muy rica). Examples Spanish English Soy de Colombia, pero estoy en Madrid. I'm from Colombia, but I'm in Madrid. Laura es médica. Hoy está cansada. Laura is a doctor. Today she's tired. La casa es grande y está en el centro. The house is big and it's downtown. Mi hermano es muy alegre. My brother is a very cheerful person. ¿Estás bien? —No, estoy un poco mal. Are you OK? —No, I'm a bit unwell. La película es aburrida. The movie is boring. Los niños están aburridos. The children are bored. ¿Estás listo? Son las ocho. Are you ready? It's eight o'clock. Good to know Estar + muerto (dead) is the normal form, although death is permanent: El pez está muerto. Some words take only one verb: ser + nouns (Es profesor), estar + de acuerdo (Estoy de acuerdo, I agree). A quick check: if you can add \"right now\" without changing the sense, estar is usually the answer. Common mistakes ✗ Soy cansado. → ✓ Estoy cansado. — feeling tired is a state now: estar. ✗ Está médico. → ✓ Es médico. — a job is what someone is: ser. ✗ Madrid es en España. → ✓ Madrid está en España. — location of places takes estar. ✗ La fiesta está en mi casa. → ✓ La fiesta es en mi casa. — where an event takes place uses ser. ✗ Estoy aburrido (meaning \"I'm a boring person\") → ✓ Soy aburrido. — with estar it means \"I'm bored\". Practice Ser or estar? Write the right form. Mi madre profesora. Hoy (yo) muy cansado. Lima en Perú. La fiesta en el bar de Juan. Carlos de Argentina. ¿Dónde los niños? Translate into Spanish. The children are bored. Are you ready? (to a friend; you are talking to a woman) Answers es estoy está es es están Los niños están aburridos. ¿Estás lista?","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-ser-vs-estar-basics"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-home-city","group":"pages","title":"Home, furniture and places in town","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Rooms, furniture and the places you need in a town, with hay and estar to say what there is and where it is.","text":"With hay and estar you can now describe where you live: Mi casa tiene dos dormitorios. Hay un supermercado en la esquina. La farmacia está al lado del banco. Learn every noun with its article. Words for \"apartment\" differ by country, so both are given; you will be understood with either. Words The home Spanish English la casa the house, the home el piso (Spain) / el departamento (Mexico, Argentina, Chile) / el apartamento (understood everywhere) the apartment la habitación / el cuarto the room, the bedroom el dormitorio the bedroom el salón (Spain) / la sala (Latin America) the living room la cocina the kitchen el baño the bathroom el jardín the garden, the yard la ventana the window la puerta the door Furniture Spanish English la cama the bed la mesa the table la silla the chair el sofá the sofa el armario the closet, the wardrobe la estantería the bookcase, the shelves la lámpara the lamp la nevera (Spain) / el refrigerador (Latin America) the fridge Places in town Spanish English la calle the street la plaza the square el banco the bank la farmacia the pharmacy el supermercado the supermarket la tienda the store el restaurante the restaurant el hospital the hospital la estación (de tren) the (train) station la parada de autobús the bus stop el parque the park el museo the museum la biblioteca the library Where? Spanish English cerca (de) / lejos (de) near / far (from) al lado de next to enfrente de opposite entre between en la esquina on the corner Word partners Spanish English Vivo en un piso pequeño. (Spain) I live in a small apartment. La cocina es muy grande. The kitchen is very big. En mi habitación hay una cama y una mesa. In my room there's a bed and a table. ¿Hay un banco por aquí? Is there a bank around here? La farmacia está al lado del banco. The pharmacy is next to the bank. El museo está enfrente del parque. The museum is opposite the park. La estación está lejos del centro. The station is far from downtown. La parada de autobús está en la esquina. The bus stop is on the corner. Mi casa está cerca de la playa. My house is near the beach. Good to know De + el always becomes del: al lado del banco, cerca del parque (but cerca de la plaza). Librería is a bookstore; a library is la biblioteca. El baño is the bathroom at home; in a restaurant people often ask ¿Dónde está el baño? or, in Spain, ¿Dónde están los servicios? Common mistakes ✗ Hay la farmacia en la plaza. → ✓ La farmacia está en la plaza. — a known place (la) takes está. ✗ la librería (meaning \"the library\") → ✓ la biblioteca — librería means \"bookstore\". ✗ al lado de el banco → ✓ al lado del banco — de + el becomes del. ✗ El hospital está cerca la estación. → ✓ El hospital está cerca de la estación. — cerca needs de before a place. Practice Write the word with el or la. kitchen bed pharmacy library Translate into Spanish. Is there a supermarket near here? The bank is next to the park. My apartment has two bedrooms. (Spain) The station is far from downtown. Answers la cocina la cama la farmacia la biblioteca ¿Hay un supermercado cerca? / ¿Hay un supermercado cerca de aquí? / ¿Hay un supermercado por aquí? El banco está al lado del parque. Mi piso tiene dos dormitorios. / Mi piso tiene dos habitaciones. La estación está lejos del centro.","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-home-city"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-gustar","group":"pages","title":"gustar and similar verbs","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Say what you like and don't like. In Spanish the thing you like is the subject, not you.","text":"In English you say \"I like coffee\", and I is the subject. Spanish turns the sentence around: Me gusta el café is, word for word, \"Coffee pleases me\". The thing you like is the subject; you are the person it pleases. That is why you only need two forms of the verb: gusta and gustan. How it works (a + person) + pronoun + gusta / gustan + what you like Who likes it Pronoun One thing, or an action Several things (a mí) me gusta gustan (a ti) te gusta gustan (a él, a ella, a usted) le gusta gustan (a nosotros, a nosotras) nos gusta gustan (a vosotros, a vosotras) (Spain) os gusta gustan (a ellos, a ellas, a ustedes) les gusta gustan gusta + singular noun with its article: Me gusta el café. gusta + infinitive, also with two or more infinitives: Me gusta bailar. Me gusta cantar y bailar. gustan + plural noun: Me gustan los gatos. Negative: no goes before the pronoun: No me gusta el té. Questions keep the same word order: ¿Te gusta el fútbol? When to use it Say what you like or don't like. Me gusta la música. (I like music.) No me gustan los lunes. (I don't like Mondays.) Ask someone. To a friend: ¿Te gusta cocinar? Formal: ¿Le gusta la habitación? Say how much. Me gusta mucho el cine. (I like movies a lot.) No me gusta nada el frío. (I don't like the cold at all.) Me encanta means \"I love\": Me encanta esta ciudad. Make clear who. Le can mean he, she or you (formal), so add a + the person: A María le gusta el cine. A mí no. Agree or disagree. —Me gusta el café. —A mí también. (Me too.) —No me gusta el té. —A mí tampoco. (Me neither.) Examples Spanish English Me gusta el chocolate. I like chocolate. ¿Te gustan los perros? Do you like dogs? No nos gusta trabajar los sábados. We don't like working on Saturdays. A Carlos le gusta mucho viajar. Carlos really likes traveling. A mis padres les gustan las películas antiguas. My parents like old movies. Me encanta esta ciudad. I love this city. ¿Os gusta la comida mexicana? (Spain) Do you all like Mexican food? —No me gusta el frío. —A mí tampoco. \"I don't like the cold.\" \"Me neither.\" Good to know The same pattern works for encantar (to love), interesar (to interest) and doler (to hurt): Me interesan los idiomas. Me duele la cabeza. Me encanta is already strong. Do not add mucho. About people, Me gustas means \"I am attracted to you\". To say you like someone as a person, use Me cae bien: Tu hermano me cae muy bien. Common mistakes ✗ Yo gusto el café. → ✓ Me gusta el café. — you are not the subject; the coffee is. ✗ Me gusta los gatos. → ✓ Me gustan los gatos. — the verb agrees with the thing you like. ✗ Me gusta café. → ✓ Me gusta el café. — things you like in general need the article. ✗ María le gusta bailar. → ✓ A María le gusta bailar. — the person needs a. ✗ Me gusto bailar. → ✓ Me gusta bailar. — me gusto means \"I like myself\". Practice Complete with the pronoun and gusta or gustan. (a mí) la paella. (a ti) ¿ los deportes? A Ana leer. (a nosotros) No las verduras. A mis amigos cantar y bailar. Translate into Spanish. I don't like coffee. Do you like the hotel? (formal) \"I love dogs.\" \"Me too.\" Answers Me gusta Te gustan le gusta nos gustan les gusta No me gusta el café. ¿Le gusta el hotel? —Me encantan los perros. —A mí también.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-gustar"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-present-irregular","group":"pages","title":"Common irregular and stem-changing verbs in the present","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"The verbs you use most are irregular: ir, hacer, querer, poder, salir, jugar. Learn the yo forms and the three stem changes.","text":"The most useful Spanish verbs are irregular, as in English (be, have, go), but they follow a few patterns. Some have an odd yo form (hago, salgo). Others change a vowel in the stem: querer → quiero, poder → puedo, pedir → pido. And a few, like ir (to go), you simply learn by heart. How it works 1. Stem changes (in every form except nosotros and vosotros): e → ie: querer (to want) o → ue: poder (can) e → i: pedir (to ask for) quiero puedo pido quieres puedes pides quiere puede pide queremos podemos pedimos queréis podéis pedís quieren pueden piden More: e → ie: empezar (to start), pensar (to think), preferir (to prefer), cerrar (to close). o → ue: dormir (to sleep), volver (to come back), costar (to cost), encontrar (to find). e → i: repetir (to repeat), servir (to serve). u → ue: only jugar (to play): juego, juegas, juega, jugamos, jugáis, juegan. 2. Irregular yo form, the rest regular: Verb yo Example hacer (to do, to make) hago Hago la cena. salir (to go out, to leave) salgo Salgo a las ocho. poner (to put) pongo Pongo la mesa. traer (to bring) traigo Traigo vino. conocer (to know a person or place) conozco Conozco Madrid. saber (to know a fact) sé No sé. ver (to see) veo Veo la tele. 3. Fully irregular: ir (to go): voy, vas, va, vamos, vais, van. Usually with a (remember a + el = al): Voy a la playa. Vamos al cine. When to use it Saying what you want and can do. Quiero un café. ¿Puedes venir mañana? (Can you come tomorrow?) Talking about your week. Los sábados juego al fútbol y salgo con amigos. Saying where you go. Voy al trabajo en autobús. (I go to work by bus.) Asking for things. Pido una pizza. (I order a pizza.) Examples Spanish English ¿Quieres un té? Would you like a cup of tea? No puedo, trabajo mañana. I can't, I'm working tomorrow. ¿A qué hora empieza la clase? What time does the class start? Los domingos duermo mucho. On Sundays I sleep a lot. ¿Cuánto cuesta? How much is it? Mis hijos juegan al tenis. My kids play tennis. Hago deporte dos veces a la semana. I exercise twice a week. Vamos a la playa en agosto. We go to the beach in August. Good to know Jugar a + game: jugar al fútbol, jugar a las cartas. In parts of Latin America people also say jugar fútbol. Saber is for facts and skills (Sé nadar, I can swim); conocer is for people and places (Conozco a Ana). Before a person, conocer takes a. Pedir is \"to ask for\" something; preguntar is \"to ask\" a question. Common mistakes ✗ Yo quero un café. → ✓ Yo quiero un café. — querer changes e to ie in the stem. ✗ Nosotros quieremos. → ✓ Nosotros queremos. — no stem change in nosotros and vosotros. ✗ Yo hace la cena. → ✓ Yo hago la cena. — the yo form of hacer is hago. ✗ Yo sabo. → ✓ Yo sé. — the yo form of saber is sé. ✗ Conozco que es tarde. → ✓ Sé que es tarde. — facts take saber; conocer is for people and places. Practice Complete with the right form. Yo no (poder) venir hoy. ¿Tú (querer) un café? Nosotros (dormir) ocho horas. Yo (hacer) la cena. Ellos (ir) a la playa. Translate into Spanish. I'm going out with my friends. (salir) What time does the movie start? (empezar) I don't know. Answers puedo quieres dormimos hago van Salgo con mis amigos. ¿A qué hora empieza la película? No sé.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-present-irregular"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-reflexive-routine","group":"pages","title":"Reflexive verbs for the daily routine","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Me levanto, me ducho, me acuesto. Reflexive verbs carry a small pronoun that changes with the person.","text":"Many everyday actions are reflexive in Spanish: you do them to yourself. English often says \"I get up\", \"I shower\", \"I go to bed\" with no extra word; Spanish adds a small pronoun: me levanto, me ducho, me acuesto. You already know one reflexive verb: llamarse (me llamo Ana). In the dictionary these verbs end in -se: levantarse, ducharse. How it works The pronoun (me, te, se, nos, os, se) goes before the conjugated verb. Person levantarse (to get up) ducharse (to shower) yo me levanto me ducho tú te levantas te duchas él, ella, usted se levanta se ducha nosotros, nosotras nos levantamos nos duchamos vosotros, vosotras (Spain) os levantáis os ducháis ellos, ellas, ustedes se levantan se duchan Negative: no before the pronoun: No me levanto temprano. Question: ¿A qué hora te levantas? (What time do you get up?) Some are also stem-changing: despertarse (e → ie): me despierto; acostarse (o → ue): me acuesto; vestirse (e → i): me visto. Routine verbs: despertarse (to wake up), levantarse (to get up), ducharse (to shower), lavarse (to wash; lavarse los dientes = to brush your teeth, also cepillarse los dientes), vestirse (to get dressed), peinarse (to comb your hair), acostarse (to go to bed), dormirse (to fall asleep). Not reflexive, but part of the day: desayunar (to have breakfast), comer (to eat; in Spain and Mexico also \"to have lunch\"; many Latin American countries say almorzar), cenar (to have dinner), trabajar, salir de casa, volver a casa. When to use it Describing your day. Me levanto a las siete y me ducho. (I get up at seven and shower.) Asking about someone's routine. ¿A qué hora te acuestas? (What time do you go to bed?) Saying what you do to your body. Me lavo las manos. (I wash my hands.) Spanish uses las, not mis: the pronoun already shows whose hands. Examples Spanish English Me despierto a las seis y media. I wake up at six thirty. ¿Te duchas por la mañana o por la noche? Do you shower in the morning or at night? Mi hijo se viste solo. My son gets dressed on his own. Nos acostamos tarde los sábados. We go to bed late on Saturdays. Desayuno café con pan tostado. I have coffee and toast for breakfast. Mis padres se levantan muy temprano. My parents get up very early. No me peino nunca. I never comb my hair. ¿A qué hora os levantáis? (Spain) What time do you (all) get up? Good to know With an infinitive, the pronoun can go at the end and joins the verb: Quiero ducharme. = Me quiero duchar. (I want to take a shower.) Lunch is the main meal in Spain and much of Latin America; in Spain it is often around 2 p.m. More on times in . Common mistakes ✗ Levanto a las siete. → ✓ Me levanto a las siete. — \"get up\" needs the reflexive pronoun. ✗ Me lavo mis dientes. → ✓ Me lavo los dientes. — with body parts Spanish uses los / las, not mis. ✗ Ella me ducha. (meaning \"she showers\") → ✓ Ella se ducha. — the pronoun matches the person: se for ella. ✗ Me acosto a las once. → ✓ Me acuesto a las once. — acostarse changes o to ue. Practice Complete with the pronoun and the verb. Yo (levantarse) a las siete. ¿Tú (ducharse) por la mañana? Ana (acostarse) tarde. Nosotros (despertarse) temprano. Translate into Spanish. I get dressed. What time do you go to bed? (to a friend) My parents get up at six. I brush my teeth. Answers me levanto te duchas se acuesta nos despertamos Me visto. ¿A qué hora te acuestas? Mis padres se levantan a las seis. Me lavo los dientes.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-reflexive-routine"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-time-dates","group":"pages","title":"Tell the time, give dates and talk about schedules","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Ask and say the time, give the date, and ask when shops open and things start.","text":"To tell the time Spanish uses ser and the article la / las: Es la una. Son las tres. \"At\" a time is a la / a las: a las ocho. Dates are simple: el + number + de + month. Two things differ from English: there is no word for \"o'clock\" (en punto only stresses \"exactly\"), and in Spain times after the half hour are usually said as \"to\" the next hour (las cinco menos cuarto). In daily life in Latin America, many people say the minutes after the hour instead (las cuatro y cuarenta y cinco) or un cuarto para las cinco (a quarter to five). Key phrases Asking and telling the time Spanish English ¿Qué hora es? What time is it? Es la una. It's one o'clock. Son las dos. / Son las diez. It's two. / It's ten. Son las tres y cuarto. It's a quarter past three. Son las tres y media. It's half past three. Son las cuatro menos cuarto. It's a quarter to four. Son las cinco y diez. / Son las seis menos veinte. It's ten past five. / It's twenty to six. Es mediodía. / Es medianoche. It's noon. / It's midnight. de la mañana / de la tarde / de la noche a.m. / p.m. (afternoon) / p.m. (night) When? Schedules Spanish English ¿A qué hora abre la farmacia? What time does the pharmacy open? Abre a las nueve y cierra a las ocho. It opens at nine and closes at eight. ¿A qué hora empieza la película? What time does the movie start? A las diez en punto. At ten o'clock sharp. de nueve a cinco from nine to five por la mañana / por la tarde / por la noche in the morning / in the afternoon / at night (Latin America also: en la mañana, en la noche) Dates Spanish English ¿Qué día es hoy? What day is it today? Hoy es martes, 5 de mayo. Today is Tuesday, May 5. ¿Cuándo es tu cumpleaños? When is your birthday? Es el 12 de octubre. It's October 12. el uno de enero / el primero de enero January 1 Dialogue Two friends plan to see a movie. Lucía: ¿Qué hora es? (What time is it?) Javier: Son las seis y media. (It's half past six.) Lucía: ¿A qué hora empieza la película? (What time does the movie start?) Javier: A las ocho menos cuarto. (At a quarter to eight.) Lucía: Perfecto. ¿Y qué día es el concierto de Ana? (Perfect. And what day is Ana's concert?) Javier: El sábado, 14 de junio, a las nueve de la noche. (On Saturday, June 14, at nine p.m.) Lucía: ¡Genial! (Great!) Culture note Timetables, trains and shops use the 24-hour clock: El tren sale a las 18:30 (a las dieciocho treinta). In Spain lunch is often at 2 p.m. and dinner at 9 or 10 p.m.; la tarde lasts until it gets dark. Common mistakes ✗ Son la una. → ✓ Es la una. — one o'clock is singular: es la una. ✗ Es las tres. → ✓ Son las tres. — from two o'clock on, the verb is son. ✗ en las ocho → ✓ a las ocho — \"at\" a time is a. ✗ mayo 5 → ✓ el 5 de mayo — the day comes first, then de and the month. Practice Write the time in words. 1:00 3:15 7:30 9:45 Translate into Spanish. What time is it? The bank opens at nine. My birthday is (on) March 3. at night Answers Es la una. Son las tres y cuarto. Son las siete y media. Son las diez menos cuarto. / Son las nueve y cuarenta y cinco. ¿Qué hora es? El banco abre a las nueve. Mi cumpleaños es el 3 de marzo. por la noche / en la noche / de noche","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-time-dates"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-food-shopping","group":"pages","title":"Food, drink, shops and prices","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Everyday food and drink, the shops that sell them, quantities and how to ask what something costs.","text":"Food is the easiest place to practice: you buy it every day. Learn each noun with its article, and notice two habits. Spanish talks about food in general with the article (Me gusta el pan), but asks for an amount without it (Quiero pan). And prices use costar (o → ue): ¿Cuánto cuesta? for one thing, ¿Cuánto cuestan? for several. Words Food Spanish English el pan bread el arroz rice la pasta pasta la carne meat el pollo chicken el pescado fish (as food) el huevo egg el queso cheese el jamón ham la fruta fruit la manzana apple el plátano (Spain, Mexico) / la banana, el banano (other parts of Latin America) banana la naranja orange el tomate tomato la patata (Spain) / la papa (Latin America) potato la verdura / las verduras vegetables la ensalada salad Drinks Spanish English el agua (f.) water el café / el café con leche coffee / coffee with milk el té tea la leche milk el zumo (Spain) / el jugo (Latin America) juice el vino / la cerveza wine / beer Shops and amounts Spanish English el supermercado supermarket el mercado market la panadería bakery la frutería fruit store un kilo de a kilo of medio kilo de half a kilo of una botella de a bottle of un paquete de a package of una barra de pan a loaf of bread (a baguette) el euro / el peso / el dólar euro / peso / dollar Word partners Spanish English ¿Cuánto cuesta el queso? How much is the cheese? ¿Cuánto cuestan las manzanas? How much are the apples? Cuesta tres euros con cincuenta. It costs three euros fifty. Un kilo de tomates, por favor. A kilo of tomatoes, please. Quiero una botella de agua. I'd like a bottle of water. No como carne. I don't eat meat. Tomo el café con leche y sin azúcar. I take my coffee with milk and no sugar. ¿Algo más? —No, nada más, gracias. Anything else? —No, nothing else, thanks. Me gusta mucho la fruta. I really like fruit. Good to know El pescado is fish as food; a live fish is el pez. Many prices are said with con: 2,50 € is dos euros con cincuenta (or dos cincuenta). Spain and much of Latin America write a decimal comma: 2,50; Mexico and some other countries use a decimal point: 2.50. Papa (potato) is Latin American; in Spain it is patata. Jugo is the word for juice in Latin America; zumo is the word in Spain. Common mistakes ✗ ¿Cuánto cuesta los tomates? → ✓ ¿Cuánto cuestan los tomates? — the verb agrees with the plural noun. ✗ Me gusta café. → ✓ Me gusta el café. — food in general takes the article after gustar. ✗ un kilo tomates → ✓ un kilo de tomates — amounts need de. ✗ la agua → ✓ el agua — agua is feminine but takes el in the singular. Practice Write the word with the article. bread cheese water chicken Translate into Spanish. How much are the oranges? A kilo of apples, please. I don't eat fish. A bottle of milk. Answers el pan el queso el agua el pollo ¿Cuánto cuestan las naranjas? Un kilo de manzanas, por favor. No como pescado. Una botella de leche.","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-food-shopping"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-possessives-demonstratives","group":"pages","title":"Possessives and demonstratives","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"My, your, his, our (mi, tu, su, nuestro) and this, that, that over there (este, ese, aquel).","text":"You have already used mi and mis in sentences like Mi hermana es alta. This page shows the whole set: mi, tu, su, nuestro, vuestro. They agree with the thing owned, not with the owner: mis libros (my books), nuestra casa (our house). It also shows the words for \"this\" and \"that\". Spanish has three distances instead of two: este (this, near me), ese (that, near you) and aquel (that, far from both of us). How it works Possessives (before the noun): Owner one thing several things yo mi casa mis libros tú tu casa tus libros él, ella, usted su casa sus libros nosotros, nosotras nuestro coche / nuestra casa nuestros amigos / nuestras amigas vosotros, vosotras (Spain) vuestro coche / vuestra casa vuestros amigos / vuestras amigas ellos, ellas, ustedes su casa sus libros Mi, tu, su change only for the plural (mis, tus, sus). Nuestro and vuestro also change for the feminine. Tu (your) has no accent; tú (you) has one. Su can mean his, her, its, your (formal) or their. If it is unclear, use de + the person: la casa de Ana, el coche de ustedes. Demonstratives (they agree with the noun): near me near you far from both masculine este libro ese libro aquel libro feminine esta mesa esa mesa aquella mesa masculine plural estos libros esos libros aquellos libros feminine plural estas mesas esas mesas aquellas mesas Without a noun they mean \"this one\", \"that one\": Quiero este. (I want this one.) Esto, eso (and aquello, far away) refer to something unknown or an idea: ¿Qué es esto? (What is this?) Eso es verdad. (That's true.) Aquí (here), ahí (there, near you) and allí (over there) match the three distances. When to use it Talking about your things and people. Mi casa es pequeña. Mis padres viven en Lima. Asking about someone else's. ¿Cómo se llama tu perro? ¿Dónde está su hotel, señora? Pointing in a shop or a café. ¿Cuánto cuesta esta camisa? Quiero ese pastel. Something far away. ¿Ves aquella casa? Es de mis abuelos. (Do you see that house? It's my grandparents'.) An unknown thing or an idea. ¿Qué es eso? (What's that?) Examples Spanish English Mi madre es profesora y mis hermanos son estudiantes. My mother is a teacher and my brothers and sisters are students. ¿Es tu coche? Is it your car? Nuestra casa está cerca del centro. Our house is near downtown. Señor López, ¿es su maleta? Mr. López, is it your suitcase? Este café está muy bueno. This coffee is very good. ¿Te gustan esas fotos? Do you like those photos (near you)? Aquellos edificios son nuevos. Those buildings (over there) are new. ¿Qué es esto? What's this? Good to know With body parts and clothes, Spanish often uses el / la instead of mi: Me lavo las manos. Texts written before 2010 often have an accent on éste, ése; today they are written without it. In Latin America aquel is used less in speech; ese often covers both distances. Common mistakes ✗ mi libros → ✓ mis libros — the possessive agrees with the things, which are plural. ✗ nuestro casa → ✓ nuestra casa — nuestro agrees with the feminine casa. ✗ Esto libro es bueno. → ✓ Este libro es bueno. — before a noun, use este, not esto. ✗ la mi casa → ✓ mi casa — no article before mi, tu, su. Practice Write the possessive. (yo) amigos (nosotros) casa (tú) hermana (ellos) padres Write the right form of este or ese. (this) mesa (these) libros (that, near you) chaqueta Translate: What's that? Answers mis amigos nuestra casa tu hermana sus padres esta mesa estos libros esa chaqueta ¿Qué es eso?","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-possessives-demonstratives"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-prepositions-place-time","group":"pages","title":"Basic prepositions of place and time","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"en, a, de, con, entre, desde, hasta, para; with places, times, days, months and seasons.","text":"A few short words do most of the work: en, a, de, con, and they do not match English one to one. En covers \"in\", \"on\" and \"at\" for places (en casa, en la mesa), but \"at\" a clock time is a (a las tres). And with days Spanish uses no preposition at all: el lunes means \"on Monday\". Learn them in phrases, not as single words. How it works Place Spanish English Example en in, on, at (a place) en casa, en la mesa, en el trabajo a to (movement) Voy a Madrid. Voy al banco. de from, of Soy de Perú. el libro de Ana desde … hasta from … to desde Lima hasta Cusco entre between entre el banco y la farmacia con / sin with / without café con leche, sin azúcar para for, to (a purpose, a person) un regalo para ti (a present for you) Location phrases with de: cerca de, lejos de, al lado de, enfrente de (opposite), delante de (in front of), detrás de (behind), encima de (on top of), debajo de (under). Remember de + el = del. Time Spanish English a las tres at three por la mañana / tarde / noche (Latin America also: en la mañana) in the morning / afternoon / at night el lunes / los lunes on Monday / on Mondays en julio, en verano, en 2026 in July, in summer, in 2026 de nueve a cinco from nine to five desde las ocho / hasta las ocho from (since) eight / until eight antes de / después de comer before / after eating After a preposition (but not entre: entre tú y yo), the pronouns yo and tú become mí and ti: para mí, para ti. With con: conmigo, contigo. When to use it Where something is: en. Las llaves están en la mesa. (The keys are on the table.) Where you go: a. Vamos a la playa. (We're going to the beach.) Where something comes from: de. El tren de Sevilla llega a las diez. When: a las, por la, el, en. Trabajo por la mañana. En agosto no trabajo. Who with or for: con, para. ¿Vienes conmigo? Este regalo es para ti. Examples Spanish English Estoy en el trabajo. I'm at work. Voy a la oficina en autobús. I go to the office by bus. La clase es de diez a doce. The class is from ten to twelve. Nos vemos el sábado por la tarde. See you on Saturday afternoon. En invierno hace frío. It's cold in winter. El gato está debajo de la cama. The cat is under the bed. ¿Quieres venir conmigo? Do you want to come with me? Me lavo los dientes después de comer. I brush my teeth after eating. Good to know Means of transport take en: en coche, en tren, en avión. But \"on foot\" is a pie or caminando. Common mistakes ✗ en el lunes → ✓ el lunes — no preposition with days. ✗ en las tres → ✓ a las tres — \"at\" a clock time is a. ✗ Voy en la playa. → ✓ Voy a la playa. — movement to a place takes a. ✗ con yo → ✓ conmigo — \"with me\" is one word. ✗ para tú → ✓ para ti — after a preposition tú becomes ti. Practice Write the preposition or the article. Vivo Madrid. Voy la universidad. La tienda abre las nueve. Trabajo la mañana. Mi cumpleaños es mayo. Nos vemos viernes. Translate into Spanish. with me a coffee with milk and no sugar Answers en a a por en el (no preposition, but the article: nos vemos el viernes) conmigo un café con leche sin azúcar / un café con leche y sin azúcar","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-prepositions-place-time"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-ir-a-infinitive","group":"pages","title":"ir a + infinitive: plans","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Voy a visitar a mi madre. Talk about plans and intentions with ir a + an infinitive, like English \"going to\".","text":"You already know ir (to go): voy, vas, va, vamos, vais, van. Put a and an infinitive after it and you have the easiest way to talk about the future: Voy a estudiar (I'm going to study). It works just like English \"going to\", so it is the first future form most learners use. Vamos a + infinitive can also mean \"let's\": Vamos a ver. (Let's see.) How it works ir (present) + a + infinitive Person ir a + infinitive English yo voy a comer I'm going to eat tú vas a comer you're going to eat él, ella, usted va a comer he / she is going to eat, you're going to eat nosotros, nosotras vamos a comer we're going to eat vosotros, vosotras (Spain) vais a comer you're going to eat ellos, ellas, ustedes van a comer they're going to eat, you're going to eat Only ir changes; a and the infinitive stay the same. Negative: No voy a salir. (I'm not going to go out.) Question: ¿Qué vas a hacer mañana? (What are you going to do tomorrow?) Reflexive verbs: the pronoun goes before ir or joins the infinitive: Me voy a acostar. = Voy a acostarme. Time words for the future: mañana (tomorrow), pasado mañana (the day after tomorrow), esta tarde (this afternoon), esta noche (tonight), este fin de semana (this weekend), la semana que viene / la próxima semana (next week), el año que viene (next year). When to use it Plans. Este verano voy a viajar a México. (This summer I'm going to travel to Mexico.) Intentions. Voy a estudiar más. (I'm going to study more.) Something you can see is about to happen. ¡Cuidado! Te vas a caer. (Careful! You're going to fall.) Suggestions with vamos a. Vamos a empezar. (Let's start.) Asking about plans. ¿Qué vais a hacer el sábado? (Spain) / ¿Qué van a hacer el sábado? Examples Spanish English Voy a visitar a mi madre. I'm going to visit my mother. ¿Qué vas a hacer esta noche? What are you going to do tonight? Mañana vamos a comer en un restaurante. Tomorrow we're going to eat in a restaurant. Mis amigos van a venir a las ocho. My friends are going to come at eight. No voy a trabajar el lunes. I'm not going to work on Monday. Va a llover. It's going to rain. Vamos a ver. Let's see. ¿Vas a venir a la fiesta? Are you going to come to the party? Good to know When the object of the verb is a person, add a: Voy a visitar a mi madre, llamar a Ana (but visitar Roma). This \"personal a\" has no English equivalent. Voy a la playa (I'm going to the beach) is ir a + a place, not a plan with an infinitive. Both use a. The present can also talk about fixed plans: Mañana trabajo. Ir a puts the intention first. See the card ir a + infinitivo in the Tense & Mood Lab. Common mistakes ✗ Voy comer. → ✓ Voy a comer. — do not leave out a. ✗ Vamos a salimos. → ✓ Vamos a salir. — after a comes the infinitive. ✗ Yo va a estudiar. → ✓ Yo voy a estudiar. — ir changes with the person. ✗ Voy a visitar mi madre. → ✓ Voy a visitar a mi madre. — a person after visitar takes a. Practice Complete with ir a + infinitive. Yo (estudiar) esta tarde. ¿Tú (venir) mañana? Nosotros (comer) en casa. Ellos no (trabajar) el sábado. Translate into Spanish. What are you going to do tomorrow? (to a friend) I'm going to call my sister. It's going to rain. Let's start. Answers voy a estudiar vas a venir vamos a comer van a trabajar ¿Qué vas a hacer mañana? Voy a llamar a mi hermana. Va a llover. Vamos a empezar.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-ir-a-infinitive"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-ordering-shopping","group":"pages","title":"Order in a café and buy things","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Order food and drinks, ask for the bill, and buy things in a shop politely.","text":"In a café or a shop a few polite phrases do everything. Spanish speakers often order with a simple verb: Un café, por favor or Ponme un café (Spain, informal). The most polite way for a learner is Quería… (I'd like…), a past form used here as a soft request; you do not need to know the tense to use it. Waiters often use usted, and customers answer with usted too. Key phrases Ordering Spanish English Quería un café con leche, por favor. I'd like a coffee with milk, please. Para mí, un té. / Yo, un agua. For me, a tea. / I'll have a water. ¿Me pone un bocadillo de queso? (Spain) Can I have a cheese sandwich? ¿Me da una empanada? Can I have an empanada? ¿Qué tiene de comer? What do you have to eat? ¿Tiene algo sin carne? Do you have anything without meat? Una botella de agua sin gas / con gas. A bottle of still / sparkling water. The waiter asks Spanish English ¿Qué va a tomar? What will you have? ¿Algo más? Anything else? ¿Para tomar aquí o para llevar? For here or to go? Paying Spanish English La cuenta, por favor. The check, please. ¿Cuánto es? How much is it? ¿Me cobra, por favor? Can I pay, please? ¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta? Can I pay by card? En efectivo. In cash. In a shop (learn these as fixed phrases) Spanish English Estoy buscando una camiseta. I'm looking for a T-shirt. (playera in Mexico, remera in Argentina) ¿Cuánto cuesta esta? How much is this one? ¿La tiene en azul? Do you have it in blue? Me la llevo. I'll take it. Solo estoy mirando, gracias. I'm just looking, thanks. Dialogue In a café in the morning. Waiter: Buenos días. ¿Qué va a tomar? (Good morning. What will you have?) Laura: Quería un café con leche y una tostada, por favor. (I'd like a coffee with milk and a piece of toast, please.) Waiter: ¿Con tomate o con mantequilla? (With tomato or with butter?) Laura: Con tomate. (With tomato.) Waiter: Muy bien. ¿Algo más? (Very well. Anything else?) Laura: No, nada más, gracias. (No, nothing else, thanks.) Waiter: Muy bien. (Very well.) Laura: La cuenta, por favor. ¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta? (The check, please. Can I pay by card?) Waiter: Sí, claro. Son tres euros con cincuenta. (Yes, of course. It's three euros fifty.) Culture note A bocadillo in Spain is a sandwich in a baguette; a sándwich is made with sliced bread. In Mexico a similar sandwich is a torta. In Spain you often pay at the end at the bar, and tips are small; in Mexico about 10–15% is usual. Por favor and gracias are used less often than in English in quick exchanges (especially in Spain); a friendly tone does the same job. Common mistakes ✗ Quiero tener un café. → ✓ Quería un café. / Un café, por favor. — you just name what you want. ✗ Para yo, un café. → ✓ Para mí, un café. — after para, yo becomes mí. ✗ ¿Puedo pagar por tarjeta? → ✓ ¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta? — you pay con a card, en cash. ✗ Una agua, por favor. → ✓ Un agua, por favor. — agua is feminine but takes un / el. Practice Write what you say. You want a tea. (polite) You want the check. You ask if you can pay by card. The waiter says ¿Algo más? You don't want anything else. Complete the dialogue. —¿Qué va a ? —Un café, por favor. —¿Para tomar aquí o para ? —Para tomar aquí. —¿Cuánto ? —Son dos euros. Translate: I'm just looking, thanks. Answers Quería un té, por favor. / Un té, por favor. La cuenta, por favor. ¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta? No, nada más, gracias. tomar llevar es Solo estoy mirando, gracias.","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-ordering-shopping"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-asking-to-repeat","group":"pages","title":"Survival phrases: ask people to repeat, slow down, explain","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"The phrases that keep a conversation going when you don't understand: repeat, slower, how do you say, what does it mean.","text":"Real Spanish is fast, and you will not understand everything. That is normal, and these phrases are your safety net. They let you keep talking instead of going silent. Most of them use poder (can): ¿Puede repetir? (formal) or ¿Puedes repetir? (informal). Learn both versions, and keep the formal one for strangers, shops and offices. Key phrases When you don't understand Spanish English Perdón, no entiendo. Sorry, I don't understand. ¿Puede repetir, por favor? / ¿Puedes repetir? Can you repeat that, please? Más despacio, por favor. (also más lento, common in Latin America) Slower, please. ¿Puede hablar más despacio? / ¿Puedes hablar más despacio? Can you speak more slowly? ¿Cómo? / ¿Perdón? Sorry? (I didn't hear) No hablo mucho español. I don't speak much Spanish. ¿Habla inglés? / ¿Hablas inglés? Do you speak English? Asking about words Spanish English ¿Cómo se dice \"key\" en español? How do you say \"key\" in Spanish? ¿Qué significa \"llave\"? What does \"llave\" mean? ¿Cómo se escribe? How do you spell it? ¿Cómo se pronuncia? How do you pronounce it? ¿Puede escribirlo, por favor? Can you write it down, please? Checking and thanking Spanish English ¿Así? Like this? ¿Es correcto? Is that right? Ah, vale. (Spain) / Ah, okey. / Ah, ya entiendo. Oh, OK. / Oh, I get it now. Muchas gracias por su paciencia. Thank you very much for your patience. Dialogue At a train station ticket office. Clerk: Buenos días. ¿Adónde va? (Good morning. Where are you going?) Tom: Perdón, no entiendo. ¿Puede repetir, por favor? (Sorry, I don't understand. Can you repeat that, please?) Clerk: Claro. ¿A qué ciudad va? (Of course. Which city are you going to?) Tom: A Toledo. ¿Cómo se dice \"return ticket\" en español? (To Toledo. How do you say \"return ticket\" in Spanish?) Clerk: Ida y vuelta. (Round trip.) Tom: Ah, vale. Un billete de ida y vuelta, por favor. (Oh, OK. A round-trip ticket, please.) Clerk: Son veinte euros con cuarenta. (That's twenty euros forty.) Tom: Más despacio, por favor. (Slower, please.) Clerk: Veinte… euros… con cuarenta. (Twenty… euros… forty.) Tom: Muchas gracias. (Thank you very much.) Culture note Vale is the Spain word for \"OK\"; in much of Latin America people say está bien, bueno, ok or dale (Argentina, Uruguay). A ticket for a train or a bus is un billete in Spain and un boleto in most of Latin America. People are usually happy to repeat. Showing you are trying is more important than speaking perfectly. Common mistakes ✗ No comprendo nada, repite. (to a stranger) → ✓ Perdón, no entiendo. ¿Puede repetir? — too direct with a stranger: use usted and a question. ✗ ¿Qué significa en español \"key\"? → ✓ ¿Cómo se dice \"key\" en español? — significa asks for the meaning of a word you heard. ✗ ¿Puede repetir, por favor. → ✓ ¿Puede repetir, por favor? — a question opens with ¿ and closes with ?. Practice What do you say? You don't understand. Someone speaks too fast. (to a stranger) You want to know how to say \"bread\" in Spanish. You want to know what \"llave\" means. Translate into Spanish. Can you repeat that, please? (formal) How do you spell it? Can you write it down, please? (formal) I don't speak much Spanish. Answers Perdón, no entiendo. / No entiendo. ¿Puede hablar más despacio, por favor? / Más despacio, por favor. ¿Cómo se dice \"bread\" en español? ¿Qué significa \"llave\"? ¿Puede repetir, por favor? ¿Cómo se escribe? ¿Puede escribirlo, por favor? No hablo mucho español.","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-asking-to-repeat"},{"id":"summary:A1","group":"pages","title":"Level summary A1","headings":["A1 in a nutshell"],"summary":"","text":"A1 in a nutshell What you can do at this level You can introduce yourself and others, say where you are from and what you do, and ask the same questions back. You can talk about your family, your home and your town, your day and your plans, and you can order in a café, buy food and ask what things cost. You understand people who speak slowly and clearly, and you know how to ask them to repeat. Key ideas Two verbs for \"to be\". Ser says what something is (Soy profesora, Es de Chile); estar says where it is and how it is now (Estoy en casa, Estoy cansada). Endings show the person. Hablo, hablas, habla…: the pronoun is usually left out. Every noun is masculine or feminine, and articles and adjectives agree with it: la casa blanca, los coches rojos. Gustar works backwards: the thing you like is the subject. Me gusta el café. Me gustan los gatos. Ir a + infinitive is your first future: Voy a estudiar. The most common trap Translating \"to be\" word for word. Age, hunger and cold take tener (Tengo 30 años, tengo hambre), location takes estar, and a job takes ser without un / una (Es médico). How long it takes Reaching A1 from zero usually takes about 80–100 hours of guided study plus practice on your own. With 3–4 hours a week, that is roughly 5–7 months. Speaking a little every day helps more than one long session a week. These are typical ranges for adult learners; your pace depends on how much time you spend with the language. What next You are ready for A2 when you can talk for two or three minutes about yourself, your family and your day, and ask someone a few questions about theirs. At A2 you will learn to talk about the past (ayer comí, de niño vivía) and to describe things in more detail.","level":"A1","category":null,"route":"#/a1"},{"id":"cheatsheet:A1","group":"pages","title":"Cheat sheet A1","headings":["Forms","Grammar","Phrases","Signal words","Watch out"],"summary":"The forms, rules, phrases and traps of A1 on one page.","text":"A1 cheat sheet Forms Form Pattern Example ser soy, eres, es, somos, sois, son Soy de Lima. Es médica. estar estoy, estás, está, estamos, estáis, están Estoy en casa. Está cansado. tener tengo, tienes, tiene, tenemos, tenéis, tienen Tengo dos hermanos. -ar / -er / -ir -o, -as/-es, -a/-e, -amos/-emos/-imos, -áis/-éis/-ís, -an/-en hablo, comes, vivimos stem changes e→ie, o→ue, e→i (not nosotros, vosotros) quiero, puedo, pido yo in -go hago, salgo, pongo, tengo, traigo Salgo a las ocho. ir (a) voy, vas, va, vamos, vais, van + a + infinitive Voy a estudiar. reflexive me, te, se, nos, os, se + verb Me levanto a las siete. Grammar Articles: el, la, los, las / un, una, unos, unas; a + el = al, de + el = del. No article with a job: Es profesor. Agreement: el chico alto, la chica alta, los chicos altos; colors after the noun: un coche rojo. ser / estar: what it is (ser) vs where it is and how it is now (estar). Events: La fiesta es en mi casa. hay / está: Hay un banco (something new) / El banco está allí (a known thing). gustar: me / te / le / nos / os / les + gusta (one thing, an action) / gustan (plural). Questions and no: ¿Dónde vives? No vivo aquí. No tengo nada. Question words have accents. Possessives / demonstratives: mi, tu, su, nuestro; este, ese, aquel. Phrases Spanish English Hola, me llamo… Mucho gusto. Hi, my name is… Nice to meet you. ¿De dónde eres? —Soy de… Where are you from? —I'm from… ¿Cuántos años tienes? —Tengo… años. How old are you? —I'm… ¿A qué te dedicas? —Soy… What do you do? —I'm a… ¿Qué hora es? —Son las tres y media. What time is it? —It's half past three. ¿A qué hora abre? What time does it open? ¿Hay un banco por aquí? Is there a bank around here? Quería un café, por favor. I'd like a coffee, please. ¿Cuánto cuesta? / ¿Cuánto es? How much is it? La cuenta, por favor. The check, please. ¿Puede repetir, por favor? Can you repeat that, please? Más despacio, por favor. Slower, please. ¿Cómo se dice … en español? How do you say … in Spanish? Me gusta… / No me gusta… I like… / I don't like… Signal words Spanish English siempre / a veces / nunca always / sometimes / never todos los días / los lunes every day / on Mondays por la mañana / tarde / noche in the morning / afternoon / at night a las ocho at eight hoy / mañana / esta noche today / tomorrow / tonight el fin de semana on the weekend en julio / en verano in July / in summer aquí / ahí / allí here / there / over there Watch out ✗ Soy 25 años. → ✓ Tengo 25 años. ✗ Soy cansado. → ✓ Estoy cansado. ✗ Yo gusto el café. → ✓ Me gusta el café. ✗ la casa blanco → ✓ la casa blanca ✗ en el lunes → ✓ el lunes","level":"A1","category":"Cheat sheet","route":"#/a1/cheatsheet"},{"id":"review","group":"exercises","title":"Flashcards and mistakes","headings":[],"summary":"One review queue: deck cards, My mistakes and weak spots from exercises.","text":"flashcards review decks mistakes weak spots","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review"},{"id":"review:words","group":"exercises","title":"My words","headings":[],"summary":"Words and phrases you selected in texts, with their sentence and meaning.","text":"my words personal vocabulary flashcards","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/words"},{"id":"deck:vocab-a1-food-shopping","group":"exercises","title":"Food, drink and shopping (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"Sample of 8 cards. The full deck has 30–40 cards for the topic a1-vocabulary-food-shopping.","text":"flashcards deck Food, drink and shopping A1","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/vocab-a1-food-shopping"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-gustar-1","group":"exercises","title":"gustar: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: choose the form, fill the gaps, build sentences and translate.","text":"Topic: gustar and similar verbs","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-gustar-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-gustar-2","group":"exercises","title":"gustar: write it yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: type the missing word, rewrite sentences, write what you hear and build a question.","text":"Topic: gustar and similar verbs","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-gustar-2"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-gustar-3","group":"exercises","title":"gustar: agree, disagree and say how much","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: listen and write, rewrite, find the mistake, match reactions and fill a table with encantar.","text":"Topic: gustar and similar verbs","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-gustar-3"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation-1","group":"exercises","title":"Sounds and spelling: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: stress, special letters, accents and question marks.","text":"Topic: Alphabet, pronunciation and stress","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-subject-pronouns-1","group":"exercises","title":"Subject pronouns: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: the right pronoun, tú or usted, and when to leave the pronoun out.","text":"Topic: Subject pronouns; tú vs usted","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-subject-pronouns-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-ser-1","group":"exercises","title":"ser: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: the forms of ser, origin, jobs and descriptions.","text":"Topic: ser: identity, origin and profession","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-ser-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-gender-number-1","group":"exercises","title":"Gender and plurals: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: el or la, plural endings and the usual exceptions.","text":"Topic: Gender and number of nouns","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-gender-number-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-articles-1","group":"exercises","title":"Articles: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: el, la, un, una, al and del, and when to use no article.","text":"Topic: Definite and indefinite articles; al and del","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-articles-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-adjective-agreement-1","group":"exercises","title":"Adjectives: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: agreement, position, nationalities and buen / gran.","text":"Topic: Adjective agreement and position","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-adjective-agreement-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-tener-1","group":"exercises","title":"tener: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: the forms of tener, age, family and tener hambre / frío.","text":"Topic: tener: possession, age and common expressions","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-tener-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-present-regular-1","group":"exercises","title":"Present tense: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: the endings of -ar, -er and -ir verbs, questions and negatives.","text":"Topic: Present tense of regular -ar, -er, -ir verbs","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-present-regular-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-questions-negation-1","group":"exercises","title":"Questions and negation: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: question words, accents, no and nada / nadie / nunca.","text":"Topic: Question words and negation","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-questions-negation-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-estar-hay-1","group":"exercises","title":"estar and hay: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: the forms of estar, location, feelings, and hay vs está.","text":"Topic: estar for location and states; hay vs está","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-estar-hay-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-ser-vs-estar-basics-1","group":"exercises","title":"ser or estar: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: identity, origin, location, feelings, events and adjectives that change meaning.","text":"Topic: ser vs estar: the basics","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-ser-vs-estar-basics-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-present-irregular-1","group":"exercises","title":"Irregular verbs: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: stem changes, yo forms in -go and ir.","text":"Topic: Common irregular and stem-changing verbs in the present","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-present-irregular-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-reflexive-routine-1","group":"exercises","title":"Daily routine: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: reflexive pronouns, stem changes and your day.","text":"Topic: Reflexive verbs for the daily routine","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-reflexive-routine-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-possessives-demonstratives-1","group":"exercises","title":"My, your, this, that: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: possessives, demonstratives and esto / eso.","text":"Topic: Possessives and demonstratives","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-possessives-demonstratives-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-prepositions-place-time-1","group":"exercises","title":"Prepositions: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: en, a, de, por, con, para, and days without a preposition.","text":"Topic: Basic prepositions of place and time","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-prepositions-place-time-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-ir-a-infinitive-1","group":"exercises","title":"ir a + infinitive: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: plans with ir a, questions, negatives and let's.","text":"Topic: ir a + infinitive: plans","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-ir-a-infinitive-1"},{"id":"mix:mix-a1-u1","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: Sounds and first words","headings":[],"summary":"Sounds and spelling, subject pronouns and ser, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Alphabet, pronunciation and stress, Subject pronouns; tú vs usted, ser: identity, origin and profession","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a1-u1"},{"id":"mix:mix-a1-u2","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: People and things","headings":[],"summary":"Gender and plurals, articles, adjectives and tener, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Gender and number of nouns, Definite and indefinite articles; al and del, Adjective agreement and position, tener: possession, age and common expressions","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a1-u2"},{"id":"mix:mix-a1-u3","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: Everyday life","headings":[],"summary":"Present tense, questions and negation, estar and hay, ser or estar, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Present tense of regular -ar, -er, -ir verbs, Question words and negation, estar for location and states; hay vs está, ser vs estar: the basics","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a1-u3"},{"id":"mix:mix-a1-u4","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: Likes and routines","headings":[],"summary":"gustar, irregular verbs and the daily routine, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: gustar and similar verbs, Common irregular and stem-changing verbs in the present, Reflexive verbs for the daily routine","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a1-u4"},{"id":"mix:mix-a1-u5","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: Out and about","headings":[],"summary":"Possessives and demonstratives, prepositions and ir a + infinitive, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Possessives and demonstratives, Basic prepositions of place and time, ir a + infinitive: plans","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a1-u5"},{"id":"lab:presente","group":"lab","title":"Presente","headings":["Present"],"summary":"What is true now, what you do regularly, and general facts.","text":"True now Vivo en un apartamento pequeño cerca del centro. I live in a small apartment near downtown. Habits and routines Los sábados juego al tenis con mi hermana. On Saturdays I play tennis with my sister. General facts El sol sale por el este. The sun rises in the east. Happening at the moment ¿Qué haces? —Leo una novela. What are you doing? —I'm reading a novel. Fixed plans and timetables Mañana trabajo hasta las ocho. Tomorrow I'm working until eight. siempre normalmente todos los días los lunes a veces nunca ahora English says “I'm reading” for an action going on now. Spanish can just use the present: Leo una novela. Estoy leyendo is also very common, but you do not need it every time. The ending already shows the person, so the subject pronoun is usually left out: Vivo en Lima. Using yo in every sentence sounds unnatural; keep it for emphasis or contrast. Stem changes do not happen in the nosotros and vosotros forms: queremos, podemos, not *quieremos, *puedemos.","level":"A1","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/presente"},{"id":"lab:preterito-indefinido","group":"lab","title":"Pretérito indefinido","headings":["Preterite"],"summary":"Finished actions at a definite time in the past.","text":"One finished action Ayer comí con Lucía. Yesterday I had lunch with Lucía. A chain of actions Llegué a casa, cené y me acosté. I got home, had dinner and went to bed. A closed period of time Viví tres años en Lima. I lived in Lima for three years. ayer anoche el lunes la semana pasada en 2019 hace dos años The English simple past covers both one finished event and a habit: “I ate there yesterday” / “As a kid I ate there every Sunday”. Spanish separates them: comí (once, finished) and comía (habit or background). Present yo and preterite él differ only by the accent: hablo (I speak) / habló (he or she spoke). The preterite yo form is hablé.","level":"A2","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/preterito-indefinido"},{"id":"lab:preterito-imperfecto","group":"lab","title":"Pretérito imperfecto","headings":["Imperfect"],"summary":"Descriptions, background and habits in the past.","text":"Habits in the past De niña vivía en un pueblo e iba a la escuela a pie. As a girl I lived in a village and walked to school. Descriptions La casa era vieja y tenía un jardín enorme. The house was old and had a huge garden. Background to an event Hacía frío y llovía cuando llegamos. It was cold and raining when we arrived. Time and how someone felt Eran las once y yo tenía mucho sueño. It was eleven o'clock and I was very sleepy. antes de niño / de niña siempre todos los veranos normalmente mientras cuando era joven English “used to play” and “was playing” are both often jugaba in Spanish. The imperfecto covers habits and actions in progress. The imperfecto does not tell you that an action finished. For one finished event at a definite time, use the indefinido: Ayer vi a Pablo. Ayer veía a Pablo sounds unfinished, as if the story goes on. Watch the accents: hablábamos (only the nosotros form of -ar verbs), but every -er and -ir form has í: comía, vivíamos.","level":"A2","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/preterito-imperfecto"},{"id":"lab:ir-a-infinitivo","group":"lab","title":"ir a + infinitivo","headings":["going to + infinitive"],"summary":"Plans, intentions and things that are clearly about to happen.","text":"Plans Este verano vamos a viajar a Perú. This summer we're going to travel to Peru. Intentions El próximo año voy a estudiar más. Next year I'm going to study more. About to happen (you can see it) ¡Cuidado, te vas a caer! Careful, you're going to fall! Let's… (vamos a) Bueno, vamos a empezar. OK, let's start. mañana esta noche este fin de semana el próximo año la semana que viene pronto Do not leave out a: Voy a comer, not *Voy comer. After a comes the infinitive, never a conjugated verb: Vamos a salir, not *Vamos a salimos. English “I'm going to the movies” has no second verb. In Spanish that is ir a + a place: Voy al cine.","level":"A1","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/ir-a-infinitivo"},{"id":"lab:ser-estar","group":"lab","title":"ser vs estar","headings":["what someone or something is","where it is and how it is now"],"summary":"Two verbs for “to be”: ser says what something is, estar says where it is and how it is right now.","text":"Identity and jobs Who or what someone is, including their job. Origin Where someone or something comes from. What it is like Lasting characteristics: personality, looks, size, color. Events Where or when an event happens: a party, a class, a concert. Time and dates The time, the day and the date. Location Where a person or a thing is, even when it never moves. (Where an event happens takes ser.) Feelings and states How someone feels or what state something is in at the moment. bien and mal Asking and saying how someone is. *Soy cansado → Estoy cansado. Feelings and states take estar. *Está médico → Es médico. Jobs take ser. *La farmacia es en esta calle → La farmacia está en esta calle. Location takes estar. *Soy 20 años → Tengo 20 años. Age takes tener, not ser or estar.","level":"A1","category":"Contrast card","route":"#/lab/contrast/ser-estar"},{"id":"lab-tree:ser-or-estar","group":"lab","title":"ser or estar?","headings":[],"summary":"Think of the sentence you want to say with “to be”. A few questions lead to the right verb, and sometimes it is neither of the two.","text":"What does “to be” tell us in your sentence? Where a person or a thing is estar Where an event happens: a party, a class, a concert ser Who someone is, their job, or where they are from ser The time, the day or the date ser How old someone is tener What someone or something is like, or how they are  Is it what they are like in general, or how they are at the moment? What they are like in general: personality, looks, size, color ser How they feel or how they are right now: tired, glad, ill, busy estar Just “How are you?” or “I'm fine” estar","level":"A1","category":"Decision tree","route":"#/lab/pick/ser-or-estar"}]};
